    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:964465/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Substitute Lecturer in Economic History, 50%, 3 months",
      "description": "Substitute Lecturer in Economic History, 50%, 3 months Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Substitute Lecturer in Economic History, 50%, 3 months Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Lund University School of Economics and Management is one of eight faculties within Lund University. More than 4 000 students and 450 researchers, teachers and other staff are engaged here in training and research in economic history, business administration, business law, informatics, economics, statistics and research policy. Lund University School of Economics and Management is accredited by the three largest and most influential accreditation institutes for business schools: EQUIS, AMBA and AACSB. Only just over 100 business schools in the world have achieved this prestigious Triple Crown accreditation. The Department of Economic History is a research-intensive department employing approximately 70 people: researchers, teachers, technical/administrative staff and doctoral students. The Department has an extensive doctoral programme and coordinates two international Master’s programmes. The Department has a well-established reputation for broad-ranging research with an emphasis on long-term processes, using economic theory and quantitative methods as important research tools. Strong research areas include economic growth and structural change, innovation, energy and sustainability, development economics, economic demography, financial history, and education and the labour market. More information is available on the Department’s website. Lund University welcomes applicants with diverse backgrounds and experiences. Gender equality, equal opportunities and diversity are fundamental principles in all parts of our activities. Lund University invites applications for a position as substitute lecturer in Economic History. The position is for three months, starting on 30 October 2026, and is based at the Department of Economic History, Lund University School of Economics and Management. The position is 50 per cent of full time. Subject area and duties The subject area is economic history with a focus on energy transitions and environmental history. The position consists of teaching, and administrative duties may also be included. Teaching may take place at undergraduate and advanced levels and will focus on sustainable development, climate crises and energy transitions from a long-term environmental and economic-historical perspective. Teaching will be conducted in both Swedish and English. Eligibility Eligibility requires a doctoral degree in Economic History and demonstrated teaching expertise. Requirements Very good proficiency in spoken and written English and Swedish. Completed higher education pedagogy training equivalent to at least five weeks, or equivalent knowledge. Assessment criteria Lund University School of Economics and Management strives to maintain a balance between research and education in all appointments. Therefore, equal weight will be given to scholarly and teaching expertise in the assessment of applicants. Administrative skills will also be taken into consideration. In the overall assessment of scholarly expertise, a doctoral degree in Economic History and publications in journals or with academic publishers using international peer-review systems are considered strong merits, as are experience of national and international collaboration and participation in research networks. Furthermore, the applicant’s ability to engage with wider society and communicate research findings will be taken into account. The overall assessment of teaching qualifications includes higher education pedagogy training, teaching experience and teaching expertise. Experience of teaching and course development in Economic History with a focus on sustainable development, climate crises and energy transitions from a long-term environmental and economic-historical perspective is considered a merit. In the overall assessment, particular weight will be given to the applicant’s ability, based on documented teaching experience and subject-specific expertise, to contribute immediately to teaching within the specified focus area. Consideration will also be given to how the applicant’s experience and expertise may complement and strengthen education and research within the Department. Application The application must include: Cover letter CV and list of publications Description of teaching and research qualifications, maximum three pages One selected paper (working paper or publication, as a link or PDF) Degree certificates, etc. Applicants are encouraged to provide the names and contact details of a maximum of two persons who can provide references. Letters of recommendation will not be considered as part of the application. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Employment expires 2027-01-29 Contract type Part-time First day of employment 2026-10-30 Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 50 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2523 Contact Anna Tegunimataka, +46462220498, anna.tegunimataka@ekh.lu.se Union representative SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 01.Sep.2026 Last application date 22.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:962271/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral fellow in Forest Remote Sensing and Climate Risk Assessment",
      "description": "Postdoctoral fellow in Forest Remote Sensing and Climate Risk Assessment at Mgeo Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Postdoctoral fellow in Forest Remote Sensing and Climate Risk Assessment at Mgeo Login and apply Lunds universitet Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Research and education at the Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences (MGeo) addresses fundamental and applied questions on the Earth’s past, present and future climate and environment, including people’s interactions with the natural world and consequences for human wellbeing. MGeo advances and deploys cutting-edge methods, models and technologies in environmental science, quaternary sciences, bedrock geology, paleontology, physical geography, biodiversity and ecosystem science, remote sensing, Geographic Information Science (GIS), and computational science for health and environment, to study processes spanning from the microscopic to the planetary, across all time scales. Subject description Forests in Sweden face growing climate-related pressures: intensifying droughts, more frequent and damaging storms, and cascading risks such as bark-beetle outbreaks. Forest owners and regional authorities need spatially detailed, timely information on where climate stress is emerging, and which stands are most exposed, but current monitoring relies largely on field inspections and coarse regional assessments that are often too slow or too limited for operational decision-making. The Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences announces a postdoctoral position within a project that addresses this gap. The project aims to develop an operational, web-based service that uses Landsat and Sentinel satellite observations, tree-species maps, canopy structure data, and climate indicators to provide continuous climate-risk assessments for Sweden's forests. The service will deliver drought-stress maps by species, a storm-exposure hazard index, and bark-beetle susceptibility signals, designed in collaboration with regional authorities and forest owners associations. As part of this work, the project will investigate the spatial and temporal mechanisms linking windthrow, drought, and bark beetle outbreaks, providing the scientific foundation for the service. Work duties The main duties involved in a postdoctoral position are to conduct research. Teaching may also be included depending on the progress of the postdoctoral project but must not take up more than 20% of working hours. In connection to this, the position includes the opportunity for three weeks of training in higher education teaching and learning. The postdoctoral fellow will: Develop and maintain harmonized satellite time-series datasets (Landsat and Sentinel-2) and integrate them with ancillary data including airborne lidar, tree-species maps, and gridded climate fields. Produce spatially explicit climate risk layers, including drought-stress maps by species, a storm-exposure hazard index, and bark-beetle susceptibility signals. Contribute to the development of an operational web-based monitoring service, including automated update workflows and a user-facing web GIS dashboard. Investigate the forest, landscape, and climate conditions that drive storm susceptibility, using major windstorms as natural experiments and interpretable machine-/deep-learning models. Publish research results in high-quality international journals (at least two peer-reviewed papers are expected). Eligibility Appointment to a postdoctoral position at Lund University requires that the applicant has a PhD, or an international degree deemed equivalent to a PhD, within the subject of the position, completed no more than three years before the date of the employment decision. Under special circumstances, the doctoral degree can have been completed earlier. Additional requirements: PhD in physical geography, remote sensing, geoinformatics, forest ecology, environmental science, or a closely related field. Demonstrated experience with satellite remote sensing for large-scale vegetation or forest monitoring, including Landsat and/or Sentinel-1/2 time series. Strong proficiency in Python for geospatial data analysis and machine learning (e.g. XGBoost), including model interpretation techniques (e.g. SHAP). Very good oral and written proficiency in English. Excellent communication skills, ability to work both independently and in a team, and to complete deliverables on time. A publication record in peer-reviewed international journals. Assessment criteria This is a career-development position primarily focused on research. The position is intended as an initial step in a research career, and assessment will primarily be based on scientific merits and potential as an independent researcher. Particular emphasis will be placed on scientific ability within the subject area. The following knowledge areas will form the basis for assessment: Quantitative remote sensing, including optical and radar data. Plant science/ecology, especially related to forest ecosystems. Computer programming. Data analysis (machine learning, statistics, numerical analysis, time-series analysis, etc.). Quantitative methods in remote sensing of vegetation, e.g. spectral mixture analysis. Other merits We are particularly interested in candidates with: Experience with web-GIS development or geospatial data visualization. Experience with high-performance computing clusters or cloud-based Earth observation platforms (e.g., GEE Python API). Experience with airborne lidar data processing and canopy height modelling. We will place great emphasis on both technical ability and personal suitability. Consideration will also be given to good collaborative skills, drive and independence, ability to clearly communicate research, and how the applicant's experience and skills complement and strengthen ongoing research within the department and contribute to its future development. Terms of employment This is a full-time, fixed-term employment of 2 years, with the possibility of extension. The period of employment is determined in accordance with the agreement “ Avtal om tidsbegränsad anställning som postdoktor ” between Lund University, SACO-S and OFR/S, dated 1st February 2022. Start date: As soon as possible or by agreement. Contact: Abdulhakim Abdi, abdulhakim.abdi@mgeo.lu.se. Instructions on how to apply Applications shall be written in English and must include the following: A cover letter (maximum 2 pages) describing (1) why you are interested in the position, (2) how you would contribute to the project, and (3) what makes you a suitable candidate. A CV that includes a list of publications. A copy of the doctoral degree certificate, and other certificates or grades you wish to be considered. Contact information for two references. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Welcome to apply! Within the Faculty of Science research and education is conducted within Astronomy, Biology, Physics, Geosciences, Chemistry, Mathematics, medical radiation physics, physical geography and Environmental Sciences. The Faculty of Science is organized into eight departments, gathered in the northern campus area in Lund. The Faculty of Science has approximately 1900 students, 330 PhD students and 730 employees. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2477 Union representative Published 28.Aug.2026 Last application date 27.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:961646/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Vacuum Technician",
      "description": "Vacuum Technician Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Vacuum Technician Login and apply Lunds universitet, Engineering I Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Your team The vacuum team is responsible for providing support to all MAX IV and its users, among other things includes: design and simulations for vacuum systems, procurement and installation of vacuum systems for the machine and the beamlines, commissioning and maintenance of vacuum systems and troubleshooting and follow up of vacuum performance for the machine and the beamlines. The team is consisting of engineers and technicians who have long experience in vacuum systems for particle accelerators. Work duties and areas of responsibility As a member of the vacuum team, your main task is to provide all the needed support related to vacuum technology for the accelerators and the beamlines of MAX IV laboratory: Perform vacuum equipment installations and testing for the accelerators and the beamlines according to standards and requests. Perform vacuum related services: cleaning of parts for use in vacuum, pumping down, leak tests, bakeout etc. Troubleshooting in case of issues with vacuum in the accelerators and the beamlines, together with the rest of the vacuum team. Periodic health check and monitoring of the accelerators and beamlines vacuum systems. Maintenance and repair of vacuum components. Keep the stock of vacuum hardware for the accelerators and beamlines in order, provide parts as needed. Orders of hardware for the stock and for beamlines with the help of the rest of the vacuum team. Actively participating in new projects and developments. Proactive support to the beamlines to anticipate their vacuum needs. Provide support in the planning and execution of the shutdown activities. Operation and maintenance of the vacuum cleaning facility. Assist with training of MAX IV employees in vacuum equipment use and maintenance. Prepare and keep documentation up-to-date, prepare technical reports. Requirements Technical education in mechanics, electronics, vacuum or relevant fields. Experience with technical or laboratory equipment. Good command in English. Taking initiative, flexible, performance oriented, communicative, ability to cooperate in a team, service minded, structured and quality conscious. Merits At least 2 years of working experience in vacuum technology, preferably ultra high vacuum (UHV). Work experience in research facilities, preferably synchrotron radiation facilities. Interest in use and development of software tools for monitoring vacuum equipment. What we offer When you join our MAX IV team, you step into a world of front edge science. We make the invisible visible by supporting scientists from all over the world, generating scientific results for the benefit of society. We offer you a multicultural work environment with great opportunities for personal development with respect for a healthy work-life balance. Would you like to work in a challenging and supporting environment? Then join us and take the opportunity to make a real difference! For further information, please visit: Work at Lund University www.sweden.se Compensation & benefits Probationary period may apply. MAX IV is a Swedish national large-scale research infrastructure hosted by Lund University. It provides scientists from Sweden and abroad, with intense x-rays and state-of-the-art instrumentation for research in areas such as engineering, physics, structural biology, chemistry and nanotechnology. Its 16 beamlines receive up to 2 000 scientists annually, conducting ground-breaking experiments in materials and life sciences using the brilliant X-rays. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Permanent position – (starting with a temporary contract) Contract type Full time First day of employment As per agreement Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 % City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2454 Contact Isabel Gallardo Gonzalez, isabel.gallardo_gonzalez@maxiv.lu.se Union representative OFR/S:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds, 046-2229364, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 26.Aug.2026 Last application date 27.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:938509/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Distributed Large-scale Software Systems",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer in Distributed Large-scale Software Systems Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Senior Lecturer in Distributed Large-scale Software Systems Login and apply Lund University, LTH, Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position is placed at the Department of Computer Science ( https://cs.lth.se/en/ ) and will be part of the strategic research area ELLIIT, Excellence Center at Linköping - Lund in Information Technology ( https://elliit.se ), a partnership between Lund University, Linköping University, Blekinge University, and Halmstad University. The holder of the position is expected to contribute to ELLIIT's broader goals within research, doctoral education, collaboration, and dissemination. The department of Computer Science conducts research in a number of areas such as embedded systems, graphics, programming languages, software technology and engineering, robotics, and artificial intelligence. The research environment is part of broad research programmes such as ELLIIT ( https://elliit.se ), WASP ( https://wasp-sweden.se ), and NextG2Com ( https://www.nextg2com.lu.se ), with excellent opportunities for collaboration with other research groups both at Lund University and other Swedish universities. The department offers a wide range of courses within the Engineering programs at Lund University, from introductory programming to specialized advanced courses and Master Thesis projects. The holder of the position is expected to give and further develop the master level course in Distributed Systems (EDAP25). We are looking for a person with broad knowledge in the field and preferably with experience from collaboration with or own previous employment in industry. Subject Distributed Large-scale Software Systems Subject description The subject of the position is the field of Distributed Large-scale Software Systems, including container architectures, distributed databases, and stream-processing software frameworks. The focus is on improving software architecture and high-level programming of such systems, supporting properties such as robustness, security, privacy, efficiency, scalability, and flexibility. Work duties Work duties include: •    Research within the subject area. •    Teaching in the first, second and third cycles of studies. •    Supervision of degree projects and doctoral students. •    Actively seeking external research funding. •    Collaboration with industry and wider society. •    Administration related to the work duties listed above. •    Involvement in the management of the department and respective administrative tasks Qualification requirements Appointment to senior lecturer requires that the applicant has: •    A PhD or corresponding research competence or professional expertise considered important with regard to the subject matter of the post and the work duties it will involve. •    Demonstrated teaching expertise. •    Completed five weeks of training in higher education teaching and learning, or acquired equivalent knowledge by other means, unless there are valid reasons. Assessment criteria When assessing the applicants, special importance will be given to research and teaching expertise within the subject, assessing research and teaching expertise equally. The assessment criteria specify the aspects to be taken into account, and the level to be achieved, in order for the assessment criteria to be deemed fulfilled. The following assessment criteria must be fulfilled for appointment to senior lecturer: •    A good national and international standing as a researcher. The requirement for international experience shall be assessed with consideration to the character and traditions of the subject. •    Good teaching ability, including a good ability to conduct, develop and lead teaching and other educational activities on different levels and using a variety of teaching methods. •    An ability to supervise doctoral students to achieve a PhD. •    An ability to collaborate with wider society and communicate his or her activities. •    A general ability to lead and develop activities. Additional requirements •    Very good oral and written proficiency in English. •    Significant documented research experience (e.g. from postdoc or doctoral studies) from another university/institute or relevant experience from industry/public sector. •    Publications in internationally leading conferences or journals in the area such as SIGMOD, VLDB, IPDPS, CIDR, EuroSys, IEEE TPDS, ACM SOSP, or Usenix OSDI. •    Collaborative skills, initiative and the ability to solve work tasks independently. Other qualifications •    Documented experience of collaboration with researchers in other areas in computer science, or of interdisciplinary collaboration. •    Documented experience of development of reusable artifacts and software tools in connection to publication in the area. •    Documented experience from teaching courses in distributed software systems and/or databases. The extent to which the applicant, through their experience and skills, may complement and strengthen ongoing research, undergraduate education, departmental innovation, and how they can contribute to the future development of the department will also be taken into consideration. We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme.  Read more on the University website about being a Lund University employee: https://www.lunduniversity.lu.se/about-university/work-lund-university . Instructions on how to apply Applications shall be written in English. Please draw up the application in accordance with LTH’s Academic qualifications portfolio – see link below. Upload the application as PDF-files in the recruitment system. Read more: http://www.lth.se/english/working-at-lth/to-apply-for-academic-positions-at-lth/ LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Permanent position Contract type Full time First day of employment As soon as possible Salary Monthly Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 % City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/1757 Contact Elin A. Topp, +46462224249, elin_a.topp@cs.lth.se Union representative SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2229364 OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362 Published 10.Jun.2026 Last application date 28.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:954763/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Computer Science with focus on Human-Computer Interaction and Visualization",
      "description": "Doctoral student in Computer Science with focus on Human-Computer Interaction and Visualization Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Doctoral student in Computer Science with focus on Human-Computer Interaction and Visualization Login and apply Lund University, LTH, Department of Computer Science Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position is in the research group led by Dr. Danqing Shi at the Department of Computer Science, Faculty of Engineering (LTH), Lund University. The group collaborates closely with the computer graphics group at the department (LUGG, Lund University Graphics Group).  The group conducts research at the intersection of human-computer interaction, visualization, and artificial intelligence. You will join a growing research team and collaborate with leading researchers at international institutions. Being a doctoral student As a doctoral student, you are both admitted as a student and employed at Lund University. As a doctoral student, you will be trained in a scientific approach. In short, you will be trained to think critically and analytically, to solve problems independently using the right methods, and to develop an awareness of research ethics. In addition, you will have the opportunity to work on projects, to develop your leadership and pedagogical skills. Throughout your studies, you will be guided by supervisors. Doctoral studies end with a thesis and a doctoral degree. More about being a doctoral student at LTH on lth.se . Subject and project description As AI increasingly takes on autonomous tasks, the ways people interact with AI span a wide range, from deliberate actions such as prompting, rating, and editing, to spontaneous responses such as eye movements and interaction dynamics. This project investigates how human behavior can be modelled in the context of human-AI interaction. The center of the project is computational behavior modeling. As a doctoral student, you will collect and analyze behavioral data in human-AI interaction and develop computational models that capture human behavior patterns. The research focuses on the areas of human-computer interaction and visualization. It involves designing controlled user experiments and applying AI  techniques such as reinforcement learning and foundation models. Additionally, your work will include designing visualization systems that support the analysis, explanation, and communication of complex behavioral data. Work duties You will primarily devote yourself to your doctoral programme, which includes participation in research projects as well as third cycle courses, seminars and conferences. The duties also include participation in teaching and other departmental work (however, a maximum of 20% of working hours). The employment period is then extended accordingly. The employment will also include travel, for example to conferences, summer schools, or meetings with collaboration partners. The work duties will also more specifically include: Responsibility for the research within the subject area: collecting and analyzing multimodal behavioral datasets (e.g., gaze data, interaction logs) developing computational behavior models designing and conducting controlled user experiment designing and developing visualizations for analysis Collaboration with international research partners. Attending conferences to give talks and network. Qualifications To be eligible for admission and employment as a doctoral student, you must fulfil the requirements below. Admission requirements A person meets the general admission requirements for third-cycle courses and study programmes if the applicant: has been awarded a second-cycle qualification, or has satisfied the requirements for courses comprising at least 240 credits of which at least 60 credits were awarded in the second cycle, or has acquired substantially equivalent knowledge in some other way in Sweden or abroad. A person meets the specific admission requirements for third cycle studies in Computer Science if the applicant has: at least 60 second-cycle credits at an advanced level with relevance for the research topic, or an MSc in Engineering in Computer Science and Engineering, Electrical Engineering, Information and CommunicationTechnology, Engineering Physics or Engineering Mathematics. Additional requirements In order to complete the doctoral programme in question, also the following requirements must be met: Documented experience in one (or more) of the following areas: Human-Computer Interaction / Visualization / Eye tracking / Reinforcement Learning / Agentic Engineering Good ability to work independently and to formulate and tackle research problems. Good written and oral communication skills Good ability to cooperate Good knowledge of English, spoken and written Other qualifications For the doctoral programme in question, the following are considered as other qualifications: Documented experience with eye tracking and/or other behavioral sensing technologies. Documented experience working with Deep Learning/Reinforcement Learning/Foundation Model. Familiarity with Human-AI Interaction, Human-AI Collaboration, Human-AI Alignment, or related research areas. Familiarity with information visualization or visual analytics. Scientific publications or conference contributions in relevant fields. We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. More about working at Lund University on lu.se . About the employment The employment is a fixed-term employment at full time, starting as agreed. Third cycle studies at LTH consist of full-time studies for 4 years. In the case of teaching and other departmental duties, the employment is extended accordingly. Doctoral studentships are regulated in the Higher Education Ordinance (1993:100), chapter 5, 1-7 §§. More about terms of employment for doctoral students on Lund University’s Staffpages. How to apply Applications shall be written in English and include: CV and a cover letter stating the reasons why you are interested in the doctoral programme/employment and in what way the research project corresponds to your interests and educational background. Copies of issued study certificates and/or awarded degree certificates. These must confirm that you meet the general and specific admission requirements for the doctoral programme and show that you have the subject knowledge required for the doctoral programme project. Two letters of recommendation Other documents you wish to be considered (publication list, a sample project, etc.) We welcome your application. LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position First day of employment According to agreement Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2319 Contact Michael Doggett, +46462224745, michael.doggett@cs.lth.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 04.Aug.2026 Last application date 30.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:950307/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Position",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Position - WASP-HS project Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Postdoctoral Position - WASP-HS project Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Position We invite applications for a two-year postdoctoral position at the Department of Sociology of Law at the Faculty of Social Sciences at the University of Lund in the context of the WASP-HS project “Vulnerability in the Swedish welfare state”, co-led by Associate Professor Jannice Käll and Guest Professor Sofia Ranchordas. The position should ideally start by no later than January 1, 2027. Project Focus Across Scandinavia, welfare states have become increasingly digitalized and automated over the past decades. This shift has led to significant improvements in public services, streamlined administrative decision-making processes, and enhanced the majority of government-citizen interactions. However, the digitalization and automation of welfare systems have also excluded thousands of citizens who lack the capacity to engage critically with digital technology. Moreover, digital tools have not been employed to address longstanding structural problems and inequalities. A substantial body of critical scholarship has highlighted the risks of algorithmic bias, the digital exclusion of individuals who already face barriers to accessing welfare rights, and the erosion of accountability resulting from reliance on opaque algorithmic processes. Yet, many aspects remain underexplored. This postdoctoral project aims to investigate, from an empirical and interdisciplinary perspective, how digital technology both reflects and exacerbates underlying structural and systemic inequalities, shapes the politics and moral dimensions of the welfare state, and influences how Swedish public authorities perceive welfare recipients. In collaboration with other members of the project team, it will also explore—and potentially help develop—novel approaches by which Swedish public authorities seek to address these challenges, enhance citizen engagement, mitigate structural vulnerabilities, and empower individuals to navigate an increasingly automated welfare system. Responsibilities Conduct empirical research (qualitative, quantitative, or mixed methods) on how Swedish public authorities engage with citizens in the governance of welfare automation. Analyze how citizen participation initiatives address vulnerability, exclusion, and empowerment in the automated welfare state. Examine the legal and governance frameworks shaping the use of AI and ADM in welfare provision. Publish research in leading academic journals and contribute to the dissemination of findings within and beyond academia. Collaborate with an interdisciplinary research team working on law, vulnerability, socio-legal studies, and public administration. Requirements A PhD (completed or near completion) in sociology of law, law, STS, political science, public administration, sociology, or related fields. Strong and demonstrated expertise in empirical research methods (e.g., interviews, ethnography, surveys, case study research). Demonstrated interest in vulnerability, welfare state governance, digitalisation, and citizen rights. Excellent verbal and written proficiency in Swedish (C1/C2) and English; Ability to work independently while contributing to an international, collaborative research environment. We Offer A stimulating interdisciplinary research environment with opportunities to shape an emerging field of inquiry. The possibility to engage in the wide WASP-HS community and its interdisciplinary events. Funding for fieldwork in Sweden and participation in international conferences. Mentorship, publication support, and career development opportunities. Salary and employment conditions in accordance with university regulations. Application Please submit the following documents as a single PDF: Cover letter (max. 2 pages) outlining your motivation, research interests, and fit for the project. Curriculum vitae, including a list of publications. A writing sample (e.g., article, dissertation chapter). A research plan for your project (5 pages) Contact details of two academic referees. The Faculty of Social Sciences at Lund University is one of the leading education and research institutions in Sweden and operates both in Lund and Helsingborg. Sociology of law at Lund University is a subject within Social sciences focusing on the relationship between law and society, such as the tension between the formal legislation of the government and the informal emergence of norms in civil society, among other contexts. Research is conducted in fields such as gender and discrimination, AI and machine learning, children's rights, welfare, legal cultures, corruption, migration, financial security and crime. The department offers courses at all levels. The department also runs the bachelor's program in criminology in collaboration with the Department of Sociology. The Sociology of Law Department is keen to engage in cross-disciplinary collaboration and has good contacts with professionals and public authorities. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment Senast 2027-01-01 Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2189 Contact Anna Melin, HR Partner, anna.melin@sam.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 07.Jul.2026 Last application date 30.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:904656/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Doctoral student on carbon cycling of primary forests",
      "description": "Doctoral student on carbon cycling of primary forests Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Doctoral student on carbon cycling of primary forests Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Research and education at the Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences (MGeo) addresses fundamental and applied questions on the Earth’s past, present and future climate and environment, including people’s interactions with the natural world and consequences for human wellbeing. MGeo advances and deploys cutting-edge methods, models and technologies in environmental science, quaternary sciences, bedrock geology, paleontology, physical geography, biodiversity and ecosystem science, remote sensing, Geographic Information Science (GIS), and computational science for health and environment, to study processes spanning from the microscopic to the planetary, across all time scales. Subject description Our research group has mapped primary forests (forests with little to no direct human impact, also sometimes called old-growth forests) across Sweden and assessed carbon storage in the most natural forest ecosystems. We have found that primary forests store much more Carbon compared to managed forests (see: Higher carbon storage in primary than secondary boreal forests in Sweden | Science). The largest carbon store, and the largest difference was found in the soil, where primary forests stored considerably more carbon than managed forests do. This was somewhat surprising since soil carbon is often considered to be relatively stable and change slowly. It could be that most of the difference in soil carbon stems from losses in managed forests. This could be due to a variety of reasons, including ditching for drainage, site preparation, removal of carbon from the ecosystems through harvest, or other mechanisms related to the diversity and continuity of the forests. Alternatively, it is also plausible that most of the soil carbon difference stems from a larger uptake of carbon in recent decades, most likely from the 1950s and forward when CO2 fertilization and climate change has accelerated. We have known for a long time from satellites that Earth’s vegetation has greened considerably during recent decades, and northern land often stands out as a greening and model-predicted carbon uptake hotspot. Wild-fires is another potentially important aspect, in Sweden wild-fires have been reduced dramatically since the late 1800s, and it could be that primary forests have been able to accumulate much more soil carbon during the last century due to the lack of fires. The difference in carbon storage between the forest types is larger than cumulative fossil CO2 emissions in Sweden. Therefore, it is important to understand what has caused these differences. Improving our understanding of how different factors — such as climate change, CO₂ fertilization, forest fires, and various forest management practices — have influenced vegetation and soils is key to assessing how broadly applicable our unique data and results may be to the global boreal zone. This knowledge is also highly relevant for understanding and projecting future changes in carbon storage, and for evaluating what continued transformation of natural to managed forests may imply for future carbon uptake and emissions. This doctoral project aims to shed light on these questions. The work is somewhat flexible and can be adjusted to the interests and skills of the candidate, but there are some clear opportunities. One involves field data collection analysis of soil samples, especially the collection of soil samples in primary forests with varying fire history. Another path involves modelling and analysis of existing carbon storage data and information on forest management. Work duties The work involves data collection and analysis of samples from existing primary forests as well as forests that have been harvested at different points in time. It also includes studies on the effects of various management practices, such as site preparation and drainage, and environmental factors such as historical fire regimes. Overall, this part of the project can be described as geographical studies, as it is largely based on the analysis of maps and other geographic and historical information sources that are integrated into spatial analyses and statistical models. The work may also include laboratory analyses of soil carbon to provide a clearer understanding of which carbon fractions have been lost. This involves analyzing the components of soil carbon, particularly mineral-associated and particulate organic carbon. These analyses can help elucidate the processes that have led to observed differences in soil carbon stocks, as well as assess how stable and how sensitive soil carbon may be to future climate change and disturbances. These studies can contribute to improving our understanding of how forestry practices affect carbon storage and thereby enhance our ability to identify ways to avoid accelerating global climate change by reducing soil carbon losses or increasing soil carbon stocks in the future. The position is suited for a candidate with a strong interest in environmental and climate-related issues, a solid foundation in quantitative methods such as mathematics, statistics, and programming, broad knowledge of natural and anthropogenic processes affecting carbon storage, and a willingness to conduct fieldwork in remote and physically demanding environments. The project offers considerable flexibility, and the doctoral student will be part of a growing research group focused on understanding human impacts on boreal forests. Our work spans several traditional scientific fields, which offers insight into a multitude of aspects in the study of how man and environment change our ecosystems. The work may also involve participation in related studies and data collection efforts, such as the collection and analysis of biodiversity data. See https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cIw3PekWDRM for an insight into what we have done previously and what the fieldwork can involve. The main duties of doctoral students are to devote themselves to their research studies which includes participating in research projects and third cycle courses. The work duties can also include teaching and other departmental duties (no more than 20%). Other background info: https://theconversation.com/swedens-old-growth-natural-forests-store-83-more-carbon-than-managed-woodlands-new-study-277150 Admission requirements A person meets the general admission requirements for third-cycle courses and study programmes if he or she: has been awarded a second-cycle qualification, or has satisfied the requirements for courses comprising at least 240 credits of which at least 60 credits were awarded in the second cycle, or has acquired substantially equivalent knowledge in some other way. Additional requirements: Very good oral and written proficiency in English. Skills in computer-based calculations, GIS and programming A degree in Science or technical subject Assessment criteria Selection for third-cycle studies is based on the student’s potential to profit from such studies. The assessment of potential is made primarily on the basis of academic results from the first and second cycle. Special attention is paid to the following: Knowledge and skills relevant to the thesis project and the subject of study. An assessment of ability to work independently and to formulate and tackle research problems. Written and oral communication skills Other experience relevant to the third-cycle studies, e.g. professional experience. Other assessment criteria: Field work expereince Drivers license Experience of field inventory of carbon storage Experience of analysis of forest inventory data Good knowledge in GIS Good knowledge in quantitative methods Good knowledge in programming, calculation tools, and statistics Expereince in formulating and answering research questions Experience in lab analysis of soil samples Knowledge of land use history Consideration will also be given to good collaborative skills, drive and independence, and how the applicant, through his or her experience and skills, is deemed to have the abilities necessary for successfully completing the third cycle programme. The research questions that this position is focusing on involves multiple traditional scientific fields, reflected in the criteria above. We do not expect that candidates have wide experience or knowledge in all aspects, but a general interest in all of them and an attitude to pursue the question although the methods may be new is important. Terms of employment Only those admitted to third cycle studies may be appointed to a doctoral studentship. Doctoral studentships are regulated in the Higher Education Ordinance (1993:100), chapter 5, 1-7 §§. Instructions on how to apply Applications shall be written in English and include a cover letter stating the reasons why you are interested in the position and in what way the research project corresponds to your interests and educational background. The application must also contain a CV, degree certificate or equivalent, and other documents you wish to be considered (grade transcripts, contact information for your references, letters of recommendation, etc.). Welcome to apply! Within the Faculty of Science research and education is conducted within Astronomy, Biology, Physics, Geosciences, Chemistry, Mathematics, medical radiation physics, physical geography and Environmental Sciences. The Faculty of Science is organized into eight departments, gathered in the northern campus area in Lund. The Faculty of Science has approximately 1900 students, 330 PhD students and 730 employees. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position First day of employment 2026-11-01 or due agreement Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/560 Contact Anders Ahlström, 0732028487, anders.ahlstrom@mgeo.lu.se Union representative OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, kansli@saco-s.lu.se, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 02.Sep.2026 Last application date 30.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:930452/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Computational Biology",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer in Computational Biology Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Senior Lecturer in Computational Biology Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Lund University welcomes applicants with different backgrounds and experiences. Gender equality, equal opportunities and diversity are fundamental principles in all parts of the University’s activities. Find more reasons why Lund University and the Faculty of Science are right for you here and here , and learn more about what it is like to Working in Lund , Moving to Lund and Living in Lund . The Department of Biology was established in 2010 through the merger of the Departments of Ecology, Cell and Organism Biology, Biological Undergraduate Education, and the Biological Museums. The department is located at Campus North, which provides unique opportunities for pioneering education and research in the biological sciences. In total, more than 300 people are currently employed at the department, including approximately 70 doctoral candidates. The Biological Museums house one of the world’s largest university collections (approximately 12 million specimens) of animals and plants, primarily insects and vascular plants from the Nordic countries. Research at the department is organized into five broad subject-based divisions, comprising smaller research groups, and encompasses many areas within the biological sciences. The department offers several Bachelor’s and Master’s programmes in “Biology” and “Molecular Biology,” attracting both Swedish and international students. Further information about the Department of Biology is available at: http://www.lu.se/biologi . Job description Work duties The position involves research, teaching, and outreach. The appointee is expected to contribute to strengthening research and teaching within the subject area. Over time, the appointee is also expected to contribute to the department’s activities by undertaking various types of leadership assignments. The content and distribution of duties may change over time, but at least 50% of the position can include research. Research The appointee is expected to establish an independent research environment with a focus on mathematical modelling of ecological and evolutionary systems. The research should complement and relate to the ongoing research conducted at the Department of Biology , thereby strengthening existing areas through a broadening or deepening of focus. Connections to current research directions may advantageously be combined with the development of new areas of inquiry. The appointee is expected to secure external funding for their research from national (e.g. The Swedish Research Council Vetenskapsrådet , the FORMAS Swedish Research Council for Sustainable Development) and international (e.g. ERC , Horizon Europe ) funding bodies, as well as through collaborations with government agencies or organisations engaged in related issues. A start package, including financing for a PhD-student position and some equipment, will be offered by the department. Teaching The appointee is expected to contribute to teaching at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels, as well as to the development of courses and programmes within the subject area of computational biology. In the short term, the appointee is expected to contribute to teaching in, for example, Modelling Biological systems (BIOS13), Population and community ecology (BIOR69), Conservation biology (BIOR83), Evolutionary biology patterns and processes (BIOR89), Biological modelling (EXTG11) and the master’s programs in computational science with specialization in biology and/or bioinformatics (e.g. Bioinformatics and sequence analysis BINP11/17, Applied bioinformatics BIOR93). The position also includes the supervision of BSc/MSc degree projects and doctoral students. In the longer term, the appointee is expected to assume responsibility for courses, and contribute to development of the faculty’s new doctoral program in computational science. Outreach The holder of the position is expected to engage with society by disseminating information about their activities and, where appropriate, ensuring that research results are of practical benefit through the development of existing, and the initiation of new, relevant collaborative activities at the department. Leadership Responsibilities The position does not initially include any administrative or leadership duties. However, over time the appointee is expected to participate in such tasks at both unit, departmental, and faculty levels. Qualification requirements A person is qualified to be employed as a senior lecturer if they have demonstrated teaching expertise and been awarded a PhD in biology or computational science, or equivalent research expertise, in accordance with the Higher Education Ordinance, Chapter 4 Section 4. The position requires at least five weeks’ training in teaching and learning in higher education or corresponding knowledge that has been obtained in some other way. An exception to this requirement may be made in special cases. Applicants who do not possess training in teaching and learning in higher education are to undergo such training within two years of taking up the position. Teaching staff at Lund University are required to be suitable for the position and to have the general ability to fulfil their work duties well. Assessment criteria Requirements for position : The applicant must demonstrate scientific expertise in analytical mathematical modelling of biological systems. The applicant must include a research plan in their application that shows how the applicant’s research profile, and their planned research, can contribute to, complement, or interact with existing research at the Department of Biology The applicant must demonstrate a very strong ability to secure national or international research funding. The applicant must have experience in supervising undergraduate students and PhD-students The applicant must possess varied and well-documented teaching proficiency. Documentation should be provided in the form of certificates from e.g. the head of department or head of school. The applicant must be able to demonstrate good pedagogical ability and teaching proficiency. The applicant must have experience of research at a different institution after completion of PhD. The applicant must have excellent English language skills. Excellent collaboration and communication skills. Meriting factors for the position Experience with other types of computational methods, such as population simulations, machine learning, or bioinformatics, is meriting. Experience in course development and course leadership is meriting. Experience in supervising postdoctoral researchers is highly meriting. A broad international network is meriting. Ability to engage with society and communicate scientific results is meriting. Documented skill and experience in leadership roles are meriting. Personal qualities Prioritized personal qualities are creativity, strong drive, openness, perseverance, ability to share knowledge and to collaborate within and outside academia, respectful interactions with colleagues, and ability to provide constructive feedback. Further considerations In the appointment process, consideration will be given to how the applicant can strengthen the Biology Department’s profile in ecological and evolutionary modelling. Applicants are expected to present their current research as well as a research plan for the coming years, demonstrating how these activities will contribute to the department’s research. Scientific merits will be assessed primarily based on the quality and volume of peer-reviewed scientific publications. As much attention will be given to the assessment of teaching expertise as to the assessment of research expertise. When appointing teaching staff, Lund University’s Appointment Rules apply, in addition to the provisions set out in the Higher Education Ordinance. For a non-Swedish speaking staff members, language level B2* must be achieved in Swedish within three years. *Common European Framework of Reference for Language, CEFR. What we offer Lund University is a public authority which means that you receive special benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension. Read more on the University website about working with us. Design and content of the application We recommend you write your application in English, as it may be reviewed by international external experts. The application is to be completed according to the Faculty of Science’s application instructions for teaching positions . Upload the application to the recruitment system as PDF files. Please note that it is very important that you follow the application instructions. An application that is incomplete will be rejected. Within the Faculty of Science research and education is conducted within Astronomy, Biology, Physics, Geosciences, Chemistry, Mathematics, medical radiation physics, physical geography and Environmental Sciences. The Faculty of Science is organized into eight departments, gathered in the northern campus area in Lund. The Faculty of Science has approximately 1900 students, 330 PhD students and 730 employees. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Permanent position Contract type Full time First day of employment As soon as possible, or by agreement Salary Monthly Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/1437 Contact Jessica Abbott, Head of Department of Biology, +46462229304, prefekt@biol.lu.se Helen Johansson, Recruitment Coordinator, +46462223609, helen.johansson@science.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, +46462229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, +46462229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, +46462220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 02.Sep.2026 Last application date 14.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:902060/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Gynaecology combined with a position as Specialist Physician/Consultant",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer in Gynaecology combined with a position as Specialist Physician/Consultant Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Senior Lecturer in Gynaecology combined with a position as Specialist Physician/Consultant Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Anställning Anställningen är placerad vid Institutionen för kliniska vetenskaper i Malmö. Den kliniska tjänstgöringen sker vid Kvinnokliniken, Skånes universitetssjukhus. Arbetsuppgifter Anställningen utlyses för att säkerställa grundutbildningens behov inom ämnet obstetrik och  gynekologi. Den huvudsakliga arbetsuppgiften är att leda och driva utbildningen inom obstetrik och gynekologi i Malmö samt vara en av tre ämneansvariga inom läkarprogrammet. Innehavaren ska dessutom vara beredd att ta på sig ytterligare ledningsuppdrag inom ämnet på läkarprogrammet, liksom andra utbildningsuppdrag på grund- och avancerad nivå vid Medicinska fakultetens utbildningsprogram samt inom forskarutbildningen. Utöver utbildningsuppdraget ingår att bedriva forskning inom obstetrik och gynekologi samt att medverka i forskarutbildningen. Innehavaren förväntas också vara beredd att bistå ledningsgruppen i utbildningsfrågor och medverka till strategisk utveckling av både utbildning och forskning inom ämnesområdet. Anställningen innefattar även arbetsuppgifter som normalt är förknippade med en tjänst som universitetslektor, såsom administration samt interna och externa förtroendeuppdrag på nationell och internationell nivå. I anställningen ingår självständig klinisk tjänstgöring inklusive jourtjänstgöring. Även andra arbetsuppgifter kan förekomma. Behörighet Behörig att anställas som lektor enligt Högskoleförordningen 4 kap 4 § är den som dels har visat pedagogisk skicklighet, dels har avlagt doktorsexamen eller har motsvarande vetenskaplig kompetens eller någon annan yrkesskicklighet som är av betydelse med hänsyn till anställningens ämnesinnehåll och de arbetsuppgifter som ska ingå i anställningen. Enligt Lunds universitets anställningsordning måste den sökande ha genomgått högskolepedagogisk utbildning om minst fem veckor eller på annat sätt ha inhämtat motsvarande kunskaper för att vara behörig, om det inte föreligger särskilda skäl. I den händelse att innehavaren bedöms ha särskilda skäl för att inte ha genomgått fem veckors högskolepedagogisk utbildning vid tillträdet av tjänsten, ska innehavaren genomföra sådan utbildning inom två år från tillträdet. Då arbetsuppgifterna innebär att leda och utveckla samt att undervisa inom grundutbildningen på främst läkarprogrammet, är svensk eller nordisk läkarlegitimation och specialistkompetens i obstetrik och gynekologi samt mycket goda kunskaper i svenska ett krav vid tillträdet. Språkkunskap i svenska lägst nivå C2 enligt Europarådets nivåskala är ett krav för anställningen. För lärare vid Lunds universitet gäller som allmänt behörighetskrav att vara lämplig för och ha den förmåga i övrigt som behövs för att fullgöra arbetsuppgifterna väl. Bedömningsgrunder Den sökande ska ha visat pedagogisk, vetenskaplig, administrativ och klinisk skicklighet av betydelse för ämnet och vid bedömningen kommer särskild vikt att tillmätas den pedagogiska skickligheten. Den sökande ska uppvisa pedagogisk skicklighet av relevans för ämnesområdet såväl inom utbildning som inom handledning på grund-, avancerad- och forskarutbildningsnivå. Detta visas genom en god förmåga att planera, leda, utveckla, driva och genomföra utbildning samt förmåga att omsätta klinisk kompetens i utbildningsmoment. Förmåga att leda universitetsutbildning på grund- och avancerad nivå kommer att vara särskilt meriterande. Vidare ska den sökande uppvisa vetenskaplig skicklighet genom att bedriva egen aktuell forskning på hög nationell eller internationell nivå. Vetenskaplig skicklighet visas genom publikationer i internationella vetenskapliga tidskrifter, anslag erhållna i konkurrens samt erfarenhet av nationellt och internationellt forskningssamarbete. Vidare visas vetenskaplig skicklighet genom forskningsprogram av relevans för ämnet och förutsättningar för långsiktig karriärutveckling. Den sökande ska visa god förmåga till ledarskap, samarbete och samverkan med det omgivande samhället. Vid anställning av lärare gäller, utöver vad som följer av Högskoleförordningen, Lunds universitets anställningsordning samt Medicinska fakultetens allmänna kravprofil och bedömningsgrunder vid anställning: https://www.medicin.lu.se/medicinska-fakulteten-vid-lunds-universitet/jobba-hos-oss/instruktioner-sokande-till-lararanstallningar Övrig information Upplysningar lämnas av prefekt Patrik Midlöv, tel. +46 73 079 13 63, patrik.midlov@med.lu.se och av dekan Maria Björkqvist, tel +46 72 700 71 63, maria.bjorkqvist@med.lu.se Upplysningar om ansökningsförfarande lämnas av rekryteringskoordinator Robert Norrman tel. +46 46 222 44 10, robert.norrman@med.lu.se Anställningen tillsätts efter förslag från Medicinska lärarförslagsnämnden. Ansökningsförfarande Anställningen söks elektroniskt via Lunds universitets sökandeportal senast 2026-11-01. Ansökan redovisas enligt Medicinska fakultetens anvisningar för sökande till läraranställning (se https://www.medicin.lu.se/medicinska-fakulteten-vid-lunds-universitet/jobba-hos-oss/instruktioner-sokande-till-lararanstallningar under rubrik Meritsammanställning och CV). Universitetet tillämpar individuell lönesättning. Ange gärna löneanspråk i din ansökan. Lunds universitet välkomnar sökande med olika bakgrund och erfarenheter. Vi ser jämställdhet och mångfald som en styrka och tillgång. Välkommen med din ansökan! The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. The Department of Clinical Sciences, Malmö is one of the six departments at the Faculty of Medicine, Lund University. We have a strong focus on clinical and epidemiological research and also conduct an extensive preclinical laboratory research. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Permanent position Contract type Full time First day of employment In accordance with the agreed terms Salary Monthly payment Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Malmö County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/468 Contact Patrik Midlöv, Head of Department, +4640391363, patrik.midlov@med.lu.se Maria Björkqvist, Dean, +46727007163, maria.bjorkqvist@med.lu.se Robert Norrman, Recruitment officer, +46462224410, robert.norrman@med.lu.se Union representative OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, kansli@saco-s.lu.se, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 01.Sep.2026 Last application date 01.Nov.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:969074/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Doktorand",
      "description": "Doktorand Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Doktorand Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Eligibility Students with basic eligibility for third-cycle studies are those who- have completed a second-cycle degree- have completed courses of at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits are from second-cycle courses, or- have acquired largely equivalent knowledge in some other way, in Sweden or abroad. The employment of doctoral students is regulated in the Swedish Code of Statues 1998: 80. Only those who are or have been admitted to PhD-studies may be appointed to doctoral studentships. When an appointment to a doctoral studentship is made, the ability of the student to benefit from PhD-studies shall primarily be taken into account. In addition to devoting themselves to their studies, those appointed to doctoral studentships may be required to work with educational tasks, research and administration, in accordance with specific regulations in the ordinance. Type of employment Limit of tenure, four years according to HF 5 kap 7§. LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position First day of employment Förlängning Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2681 Published 14.Sep.2026 Last application date 18.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:966007/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral research fellow in Synthesis of Conductive Polymers and their Implantation",
      "description": "Postdoctoral research fellow in Synthesis of Conductive Polymers and their Implantation Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Postdoctoral research fellow in Synthesis of Conductive Polymers and their Implantation Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace Chemical Biology & Therapies is a multidisciplinary research laboratory spanning the Faculties of Medicine and Science. Our research brings together expertise in materials and medicinal chemistry, in vitro and in vivo biology, and applied physics to develop and investigate new therapeutic strategies based on transient conductive polymers. By working at the interface of chemistry, biology, medicine, and bioelectronics, we aim to translate fundamental scientific discoveries into innovative approaches for therapeutic intervention. Our research environment is highly collaborative and interdisciplinary, with team members contributing complementary expertise and perspectives. We value scientific curiosity, creativity, openness, and the willingness to work across traditional disciplinary boundaries. As a workplace, we are committed to maintaining a positive, inclusive, and supportive working environment. Respect, consideration, and professionalism are fundamental to how we interact with one another. We encourage open communication, constructive scientific discussion, mutual support, and shared responsibility for creating an environment in which all group members can develop, contribute, and perform at their best. We offer Lund University is a state authority, which means you get special benefits, generous holidays and a favorable occupational pension. We also have a flexible time agreement that creates good conditions for a balance between work and leisure. Read more on the university's website about being employed at Lund University, Work with us Job duties The primary duties of the position include the synthesis of self-assembling conductive polymers and molecules for applications in molecular electronics. Description of the project This project focuses on the design, synthesis, and characterization of functionalized EDOT-based monomers and conductive polymers for biomedical applications. The work builds on our development of the self-doped PEDOT-S derivative A5, in which changes in synthetic methodology and polymer composition strongly influence solubility, self-aggregation, and electrical conductivity, thereby enabling the formation of injectable conductive structures in physiological environments. The project will explore new EDOT oligomers and functionalized derivatives, including modification of ionic side chains and development of photoactive EDOT trimers. Synthetic work will be combined with purification and chemical characterization to establish structure–property relationships governing aqueous solubility, polymerization, aggregation, and conductivity. Particular emphasis will be placed on developing monomers that can undergo controlled polymerization under biologically compatible conditions, including visible-light photopolymerization. The previous work demonstrates that functionalized EDOT trimers can be polymerized using green or red light and photocatalysts, providing chemical control over the formation of conductive polymers. In addition, the project will involve in vivo implantation of the polymeric constructs and their evaluation for localized gene electrotherapy. Requirements for the position are: A PhD in organic chemistry. The doctoral degree must have been awarded no later than the date on which the appointment decision is made. Extensive hands-on experience in the synthesis, purification, and characterization of small organic molecules. Practical experience in heterocyclic chemistry and carbon–carbon cross-coupling reactions, including the synthesis of functional building blocks for conductive and bioelectronic materials. Practical experience in the preparation and in vivo implantation of conductive polymer constructs. Experience in gene delivery by electroporation/electrotransfer, including evaluation of gene-transfer efficiency using flow cytometry. Additional merits The following will be considered particularly meritorious: Applicants who obtained their doctoral degree no more than three years before the application deadline. Experience spanning both synthetic organic chemistry and in vivo experimental work, particularly involving conductive polymers, bioelectronics, or localized gene delivery. The assessment of applicants will primarily be based on their scientific merits and potential as researchers. Particular emphasis will be placed on scientific ability within the subject area. About the employment The position is a fixed-term, 100% role of two or three years, with a start date on 1st of February 2027 or as agreed. Employment at Lund University is based in Sweden; you are therefore expected to be able to perform your work on-site in Lund. The position is limited to a minimum of two and a maximum of three years, in accordance with the collective agreement on fixed-term employment for postdoctoral researchers (Arbetsgivarverket). A position as a postdoctoral researcher is intended as an initial step in your career and as an opportunity to further deepen and broaden your research expertise. How to apply You apply for the position via the university's recruitment system. The application should include a personal letter explaining why you are interested in the position and how the position matches your qualifications. The application should also include a degree certificate, CV or equivalent, and anything else you wish to refer to (copies of grades, details of references, letters of recommendation, etc.) About the Faculty of Medicine The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment 2027-02-01 Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2580 Contact Johanna Dahlin, HR-partner, johanna.dahlin@med.lu.se Roger Olsson, Forskargruppschef, roger.olsson@med.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 07.Sep.2026 Last application date 21.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:965291/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Project Assistant to the unit neurodegenerativ",
      "description": "Project Assistant to the unit neurodegenerativ Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Project Assistant to the unit neurodegenerativ Login and apply Lund University, Faculty of Medicine, Dept. Clinical Sciences, Malmö Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The Neurodegenerative Research group is focused on defining and understanding biological processes in Alzheimer’s disease and other neurodegenerative diseases. We analyze large datasets collected from patients and cognitively unimpaired volunteers, who undergo extensive testing with neuroimaging, biomarker measurements and neuropsychological testing. Our group applies sophisticated statistical tools to these unique and state-of-the-art datasets in order to model processes related to aging and disease. The project assistant will join a growing multidisciplinary team of neuroscientists, statisticians, biologists and engineers, who are dedicated to increasing understanding of neurodegenerative diseases. The long-term goal of our research is to contribute to the development of new treatments for neurodegenerative diseases where effective treatments are still lacking. The research group is led by Dr. Jacob Vogel, and this position is located at the Biomedical Center (BMC) in Lund. We aim to create a positive, supportive and inclusive work environment based on respect and mutual consideration. We strive to create an environment that promotes development and is stimulating to all employees. What we offer Lund University is a public authority, which means that you will have special benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension. We also have a flexitime agreement that creates good conditions for work/life balance. Read more on the University’s website about being a Lund University employee, Work at Lund University Work duties and responsibilities Recent research has highlighted the use of plasma proteomics data for simultaneously quantifying the health of various organs and bodily systems, including the brain, in living humans. Our lab is investigating the efficacy of these approaches for understanding systemic changes associated with neurodegenerative diseases. The Project Assistant will primarily focus on processing and analyzing large-scale proteomics datasets. The Project Assistant will develop workstreams and execute existing omics and machine learning-based pipelines to process and postprocess this data. The Project Assistant will be encouraged and given the opportunity to lead their own project analyzing proteomics data in the context of aging and Alzheimer’s disease. Other tasks within the research group may also be assigned. The position may also involve international travel to support training and career development. Qualifications Requirements for the position are: M.Sc. in engineering, neuroscience, statistics, quantitative biology, physics, mathematics or a related discipline. Fluency in Python programming language. Experience in statistical modeling and/or AI Experience with curation, preprocessing and/or analysis of proteomics data. Excellent communications skills in English, both verbal and written. We are looking for someone who is motivated, meticulous, and well-organized. Experience with analysis of neuroimaging data. Experience with UKBiobank. Merits for the position are: Familiarity with git and GitHub. Competence using Linux-based high-performance parallel computing systems. Other information This is a permanent position, full time (100 %) with starting date November 1, 2026 or according to agreement. How to apply The position is applied for via Lund University’s recruitment system Varbi. Applications must include a personal motivation letter in which you describe how you meet the qualification requirements and why you are interested in the position. The application must also contain a CV, contact information of two references, a diploma (including a grade transcript) and other documents you wish to be considered (e.g. letters of recommendation). The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. The Department of Clinical Sciences, Malmö is one of the six departments at the Faculty of Medicine, Lund University. We have a strong focus on clinical and epidemiological research and also conduct an extensive preclinical laboratory research. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Permanent position Contract type Full time First day of employment 1 november 2026 eller enligt överenskommelse Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2550 Contact Jacob Vogel, Rekryterande chef, 046-9743788, jacob.vogel@med.lu.se Sarah Quist, HR-partner, +4640391055, sarah.quist@med.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 08.Sep.2026 Last application date 23.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:965152/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Project Assistant",
      "description": "Project Assistant at SESAC Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Project Assistant at SESAC Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position is placed at the Department of Human Geography and attached to SESAC – the Swedish Competence Centre for Satellite Enabled Social Science Analytics. SESAC is funded by the Swedish National Space Agency and run by Lund University together with Chalmers University of Technology, Linköping University and Karlstad University. The mission of the centre is to make satellite data and Earth observation usable in social science research – on sustainability, public health, urbanisation, population dynamics and crisis preparedness – and to build bridges between academia, public authorities, industry and civil society. You will work in a small, interdisciplinary environment where human geographers, physical geographers, remote sensing specialists and data scientists work side by side, and where activities span four universities. The role is the hub of the centre: you hold the practical and administrative work together, working closely with the centre’s director and principal investigator (PI). You will be based in Lund. We work actively for a good working environment through a clear introduction, regular appraisal meetings, an open seminar and meeting culture, and a reasonable workload across the year. Read more about the centre: www.sam.lu.se/en/sesac www.sesac.se We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that you receive particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. We also have a flexitime agreement that creates good conditions for a balance between work and leisure. Read more on the University’s website about being employed at Lund University: Work for us | Lund University Work duties and areas of responsibility The position combines coordination of the centre’s activities with qualified technical work on geospatial data. You will have particular responsibility for planning, structuring and following up SESAC’s activities, in close cooperation with the PI. Your duties will include: planning and carrying out the centre’s activities – conferences, workshops, courses, webinars and hackathons – from invitations, registrations and logistics to budget, delivery and follow-up coordinating the cooperation between the four universities: convening meetings, preparing agendas and supporting documents, documenting decisions and following up on their implementation structuring the centre’s ongoing work with timelines, project overviews and deadlines, and contributing to reporting to the funder supporting the PI in daily operations with background material, presentations and summaries taking responsibility for parts of the centre’s external communication, such as the website, newsletters and social media acting as contact person for partners, visiting researchers and visitors compiling, processing and quality-assuring satellite and geospatial data, and producing maps and visualisations contributing to documentation of data, code and workflows so that they are open and reusable developing and maintaining training and demonstration material for the centre’s courses and webinars The position involves travel within Sweden and occasional travel abroad. Qualifications Requirements for the position are: a Master’s degree in human geography, physical geography, GIS/remote sensing, data science, a quantitatively oriented social science subject, or another subject relevant to the position documented experience of independently planning and carrying out activities such as conferences, workshops, courses or seminars experience of structuring and coordinating work in projects with several partners, including scheduling, documentation and follow-up good knowledge of GIS and/or remote sensing, for example QGIS, ArcGIS or Google Earth Engine experience of programming in Python and/or R for data processing and analysis very good ability to express yourself orally and in writing in English good ability to cooperate, as well as the ability to work independently, in a structured and accurate manner, and to take initiative Great emphasis will be placed on personal suitability. Additional qualifications that are meritorious: experience of working in a university or research environment, for example in research administration or project finance experience of externally funded research projects and of reporting to funders experience of working with Copernicus/Sentinel, Landsat or high-resolution satellite data experience of machine learning or AI applied to image data experience of version control with Git, reproducible workflows and cloud-based data processing experience of science communication, web publishing or editing newsletters and social media experience of international collaboration a category B driving licence Further information This is a fixed-term employment of one year at 100 per cent, starting as soon as possible or by agreement. Interviews are planned to be held at our offices in Lund during week 39/ 40. Contact: Ola Hall, Director of SESAC, ola.hall@keg.lu.se, +46 46 222 84 09. How to apply Applications are submitted via the University’s recruitment system. The application must include a cover letter stating why you are interested in the position and in what way the position matches your qualifications. The application must also include a CV, degree certificate or equivalent, and anything else you wish to invoke (copies of grade transcripts, contact details for referees, letters of recommendation, etc.). The Faculty of Social Sciences at Lund University is one of the leading education and research institutions in Sweden and operates both in Lund and Helsingborg. The Faculty of Social Sciences at Lund University is one of the leading education and research institutions in Sweden and operates both in Lund and Helsingborg. The Department of Human Geography (Institutionen för kulturgeografi och ekonomisk geografi) at Lund University is a research-intensive academic unit within the Faculty of Social Sciences. It combines long academic traditions with modern and highly internationalised research and education. The department hosts a broad portfolio of education at undergraduate, master’s and doctoral levels, including internationally oriented programmes such as the Master’s in Human Geography. The department comprises a diverse faculty. The exact staffing varies, but the department currently employs 55–60 academic and administrative staff. The department educates roughly 400 full-time students across bachelor’s and master’s programmes, and it employs about 15 doctoral students. The research environment is strongly oriented towards external funding and has been successful competitively at national and European levels. The department’s research and education activities have received very favourable assessments in internal and external evaluations, positioning it among Lund University’s leading disciplinary domains. Lund University as a whole performs prominently in global rankings. According to the most recent QS World University Rankings, Lund University is placed #72 globally and achieves strong subject-level positions, including in fields related to human geography and environmental sciences. Lund University also ranks #1 in the world for sustainability in the same ranking, reflecting its strengths in socially and environmentally relevant research and education. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment Snarast Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100% City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2546 Contact Ola Hall, Prefekt, 046-2228409, ola.hall@keg.lu.se Elin Johansson, elin.johansson@sam.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 09.Sep.2026 Last application date 23.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:967042/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Research Engineer with expertise in image processing, visualization and GUI development",
      "description": "Research Engineer with expertise in image processing, visualization and GUI development Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Research Engineer with expertise in image processing, visualization and GUI development Login and apply Lund University, Department of experimental medical science Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the Workplace InfraVis is Sweden’s national infrastructure for visualization of scientific data. Currently, InfraVis employs over 50 experts across nine universities. CIPA is Lund University’s local infrastructure for image processing and analysis. CIPA is also the coordinating unit for Lund University's InfraVis node, consisting of around 10 people with complementary expertise. InfraVis and CIPA provide all employees with professional development opportunities and strive to meet each individual’s development and well-being goals as much as possible. As a research engineer with expertise in the field of image processing, visualization and development of user-friendly Graphical User Interfaces (GUI:s) within InfraVis/CIPA, you will have access to both local and national colleagues for stimulating exchanges, discussions, and joint efforts to solve complex challenges, develop new methods, and support research in a wide range of scientific domains nationwide through visualization expertise. We offer Lund University is a government agency, which means you receive special benefits, generous vacation, and a favourable occupational pension. We also have a flexible working hours agreement that provides good conditions for work-life balance. Read more on the university’s website about being an employee at Lunds University, Work for us | Lund University . Work duties and areas of responsibility The primary task is to contribute as an expert in various research projects supported by CIPA/InfraVis. Regular and active participation in joint activities, including meetings, is required to promote knowledge transfer and infrastructure development. The position includes opportunities for career and skill development. The work will focus on image processing and visualization of images or volumes, and may involve the following areas: Image processing and image analysis of tomographic data, e.g., synchrotron X-ray microtomography or lab-based X-ray microtomography. Development of scientific visualizations. Development of user-friendly Graphical User Interfaces (GUI:s) capable of performing image processing, image analysis and visualization. Qualifications We seek an experienced and driven individual who collaborates well and thrives in a complex research environment. Requirements for the position include: Ph.D. or equivalent experience in a relevant subject. Excellent oral and written English skills. Experience in image processing and image analysis of images or volumes, e.g., in ImageJ, or Python. Experience in visualization software, e.g., Blender, DragonFly. Knowledgeable in programming, e.g., Python. Experience in developing user-friendly Graphical User Interfaces (GUI:s) High motivation, ability to quickly learn new techniques, and strong time management skills. Strong collaboration and communication skills. Meritorious for the position: • Proven ability to collaborate with researchers from different disciplines. Consideration will also be given to how the applicant's experience and expertise complement and strengthen the ongoing activities within the infrastructure and how they can contribute to its future development. Further information The position is a fixed-temporary position for 3 months at 100%. Desired start date is 2026-10-01 or as agreed. How to Apply Apply through the university’s recruitment system. The application should include a cover letter explaining your motivation and how the position aligns with your qualifications, a CV. Degree certificates or equivalent. Contact details for at least two references. Any other supporting documents (e.g., reference letters). More information about the Faculty of Medicine The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Special fixed-term employment Contract type Full time First day of employment Så snart som möjligt eller enligt överenskommelse Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2610 Contact Kajsa M Paulsson, Forskargruppschef, +46462224167, kajsa_m.paulsson@med.lu.se Tyra Lundquister, HR-partner, +46462220000, tyra.lundquister@med.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 10.Sep.2026 Last application date 24.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:963180/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "PhD student in advanced data analysis for data integration, image analysis, and precision oncology",
      "description": "PhD student in advanced data analysis for data integration, image analysis, and precision oncology Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) PhD student in advanced data analysis for data integration, image analysis, and precision oncology Login and apply Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Sciences, Lund Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The overarching goal of our research is to advance precision medicine by improving prognostication and treatment selection for cancer patients through the development of novel prognostic and treatment-predictive biomarkers, as well as by elucidating tumor biology to better understand mechanisms of treatment resistance and identify new therapeutic targets. To achieve this, we apply state-of-the-art technologies for molecular tumor analysis using both solid tissue and blood-based liquid biopsies derived from unique and well-annotated clinical cohorts. Our research integrates advanced liquid biopsy approaches, including analysis of circulating tumor cells and circulating tumor DNA, with comprehensive tissue-based profiling methods such as gene expression analysis, DNA sequencing, single-cell sequencing, and spatial transcriptomics. These high-dimensional data are analyzed using advanced bioinformatic and biostatistical methodologies. As a doctoral student, you will be part of a highly collaborative and multidisciplinary research group focusing on developing precision medicine tools for better diagnosis and treatment of breast and prostate cancer. You will work closely with computational scientists, molecular biologists and clinical researchers in a translational setting. The research is conducted within a highly supportive academic environment with excellent access to infrastructure and core facilities at Lund University, including platforms for genomics, transcriptomics, bioinformatics, and advanced imaging. Within the research group, we value a positive work environment characterized by respect and consideration in our relationships. We continuously strive to create conditions that promote job satisfaction, development, and participation for all employees. The research group is now recruiting a full-time doctoral student (100%) with a start date of 1 November 2026 (or as agreed). Description of PhD Project The PhD student will be responsible for leading projects focused on the development of novel computational and AI tools to improve our understanding of multimodal data for precision oncology. The projects include integrating different types of sequencing technologies for cell-free DNA in metastatic prostate cancer, developing novel methods to analyze single-cell spatial transcriptomics and protein data in paired prostatectomy samples and lymph nodes in prostate cancer, and to develop a novel method to classify lymphovascular invasion in primary breast cancer from whole-slide histology imaging and gene expression data. We offer Lund University is a public authority, which means that employees get benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. Read more on the University website about being a Lund University employee: Work at Lund University Work description As a doctoral student, you will, under supervision, be responsible for initiating, conducting, and completing research projects in precision cancer medicine. In particular, your responsibilities will include: Advanced data analysis, including the development of software infrastructure to optimize data workflows Developing analytical methods to integrate different types of sequencing-based DNA data. Developing computational models for prognosis and treatment prediction Integrating various types of spatial single-cell data, such as gene expression and protein expression data Developing image analysis methods for histopathological specimens using AI-based models As a doctoral student, you will also, under supervision: Contribute to various stages of the scientific publication process Present scientific results in different forums Apply for research funding Additional duties related to the position may be assigned. Qualifications Required Qualifications; A background in Bioinformatics, Computational Biology, or equivalent (at least 240 ECTS credits, of which 60 ECTS credits are at an advanced level). Experience working with AI models for image analysis Experience working with sequencing-based analysis of DNA Documented ability to work independently in close collaboration with clinicians and other researchers Experience working with spatial single-cell technologies Ability to communicate in English, both spoken and written. We expect you, as a person, to be meticulous and goal-oriented. Meritorious Qualifications are: Advanced courses in data science or computational biology relevant for cancer research Previous research projects using bioinformatic methods for cancer research, in particular development of novel tools for prognostic or treatment predictive biomarkers. Previous experience of breast or prostate cancer research. Previous experience working with liquid biopsies for cancer diagnostics. Eligibility Students with basic eligibility for third-cycle studies are those who- have completed a second-cycle degree- have completed courses of at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits are from second-cycle courses, or- have acquired largely equivalent knowledge in some other way, in Sweden or abroad. The employment of doctoral students is regulated in the Swedish Code of Statues 1998: 80. Only those who are or have been admitted to PhD-studies may be appointed to doctoral studentships. When an appointment to a doctoral studentship is made, the ability of the student to benefit from PhD-studies shall primarily be taken into account. In addition to devoting themselves to their studies, those appointed to doctoral studentships may be required to work with educational tasks, research and administration, in accordance with specific regulations in the ordinance. Type of employment Limit of tenure, four years according to HF 5 kap 7§. How to apply Applications are to be submitted via the University’s recruitment system. The application should include a CV and a personal letter justifying your interest in the position and how it matches your qualifications. The application should also include a degree certificate or equivalent and any other document to which you would like to draw attention (copies of grade transcripts, details of referees, letters of recommendation, etc.). Welcome to apply! The Department of Clinical Sciences Lund is one of six departments at the Faculty of Medicine. Clinical Sciences Lund cooperates closely with Skåne University Hospital and the Faculty of Medicine in order to optimize the conditions for preclinical and clinical translational research, research strategies and development, as well as education on both undergraduate and postgraduate levels. The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Employment expires 2030-09-30 First day of employment 2026-11-01 Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2496 Contact Lina Bull, HR-partner, lina.bull@med.lu.se Martin Sjöström, Main Supervisor, martin.sjostrom@med.lu.se Lisa Rydén, Head of division, lisa.ryden@med.lu.se Union representative OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362 SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366 Published 04.Sep.2026 Last application date 25.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:968438/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral fellow in structural biology",
      "description": "Post-doctoral fellow in structural biology Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Post-doctoral fellow in structural biology Login and apply Lunds universitet Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Subject description The project involves structural studies of diatom viruses with the aim of increasing knowledge about the virus factories formed inside diatoms. The work includes cultivation of diatoms for virus infection, characterization of infection (e.g., using electron microscopy and determination of virus titers), purification of viruses and cellular components, and structure determination using cryo-electron microscopy. Work duties The main duties involved in a post-doctoral posistion is to conduct research. Teaching may also be included, but up to no more than 20% of working hours. The position shall include the opportunity for three weeks of training in higher education teaching and learning. Detailed description of the work duties: Research within the subject area Supervision of degree projects and doctoral students Actively seeking external research funding Qualification requirements Appointment to a post-doctoral position requires that the applicant has a PhD, or an international degree deemed equivalent to a PhD, within the subject of the position, completed no more than three years before the date of employment decision. Under special circumstances, the doctoral degree can have been completed earlier. Additional requirements: Very good oral and written proficiency in English. Documented practical experience in structure determination using cryo-electron microscopy. Documented practical experience working with viruses. Documented practical experience in cultivating prokaryotic and/or eukaryotic algae. Assessment criteria and other qualifications This is a career development position primarily focused on research. The position is intended as an initial step in a career, and the assessment of the applicants will primarily be based on their research qualifications and potential as researchers. Particular emphasis will be placed on research skills within the subject. For appointments to a post-doctoral position, the following shall form the assessment criteria: A good ability to develop and conduct high-quality research. Teaching skills. Additional assessment criteria: Practical experience of in situ structure determination using cryo-electron tomography. Practical experience of non-cryogenic electron microscopy techniques. Practical experience in cloning, expression, purification, and characterization of proteins. Practical experience in supervision of students. Consideration will also be given to good collaborative skills, drive and independence, and how the applicant’s experience and skills complement and strengthen ongoing research within the department, and how they stand to contribute to its future development. Terms of employment This is a full-time, fixed-term employment of a maximum of 2 years. The period of employment is determined in accordance with the agreement “Avtal om tidsbegränsad anställning som postdoktor” (“Agreement on fixed-term employment as a post-doctoral fellow”) between Lund University, SACO-S and OFR/S, dated 1st February 2022. Instructions on how to apply Applications shall be written in English and be compiled into a PDF-file containing: résumé/CV, including a list of publications, a general description of past research and future research interests (no more than three pages), contact information of at least two references, copy of the doctoral degree certificate, and other certificates/grades that you wish to be considered. Within the Faculty of Science research and education is conducted within Astronomy, Biology, Physics, Geosciences, Chemistry, Mathematics, medical radiation physics, physical geography and Environmental Sciences. The Faculty of Science is organized into eight departments, gathered in the northern campus area in Lund. The Faculty of Science has approximately 1900 students, 330 PhD students and 730 employees. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment 2026-11-15 or as agreed Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2658 Contact Anna Munke, +46721433819, anna.munke@biochemistry.lu.se Jessica Edwinsson, jessica.edwinsson@chem.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 11.Sep.2026 Last application date 25.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:965496/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Assistant Researcher to the unit Urology Bladder Research Group",
      "description": "Assistant Researcher to the unit Urology Bladder Research Group Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Assistant Researcher to the unit Urology Bladder Research Group Login and apply Lund University, Faculty of Medicine, Dept. Translationell Medicine Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the Research Group The Clinical Urothelial Cancer Research Group (focusing on bladder cancer, renal pelvis cancer, and ureteral cancer) conducts research aimed at improving prognostic assessment through the use of biomarkers and other methods for predicting and monitoring treatment response. The group is led by Professor and Senior Consultant Fredrik Liedberg . More information about the research can be found here . The research activities are based at the Department of Translational Medicine in Malmö . Within the research group, we foster a good working environment with respect and consideration in our relationships to each other and strive for a workplace that is developing and stimulating for all employees. What We Offer Lund University is a public authority, which means that you are entitled to special benefits, generous annual leave, and a favorable occupational pension. We also have a flextime agreement, which contributes to good conditions for a healthy work–life balance. Read more on the University’s website about being employed at Lund University: Work with us Work duties and areas of responsibility In the role of Assistant Researcher, you will perform laboratory work (including cell culture in model systems) and conduct bioinformatics analyses. Responsibilities also include the preparation of scientific manuscripts, the submission of abstracts, and participation in relevant scientific conferences. You may also be involved in the supervision of students. Qualifications The following qualifications are required for the position: A PhD degree. Extensive knowledge of the molecular subtyping of bladder cancer. Documented experience in next-generation sequencing (NGS) analysis. Extensive experience of laboratory work using bladder cancer models. Documented proficiency in spoken and written English. The position requires strong collaborative skills, a solution-oriented approach, and the ability to work independently and take initiative. Consideration will also be given to how the applicant's experience and expertise are deemed to complement and strengthen ongoing research within the research groups, as well as contribute to their future development. Additional Information This is a speciall fixed-term employment (SÄVA) for the period 1 December 2026 to 28 February 2027 , at 100% full-time. How to Apply Applications are submitted through the University’s recruitment system. The application must include a personal letter describing how you meet the qualification requirements and why you are interested in the position. The application must also include a CV, a copy of your doctoral degree certificate, and a list of publications. The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. The Department of Translational Medicine is one of the six departments at the Faculty of Medicine, Lund University. We conduct basic science in a laboratory setting, point-of-care and clinical research as well as carry out epidemiological studies. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Special fixed-term employment Contract type Full time First day of employment 2026-12-01 Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2556 Contact Fredrik Liedberg, Rekryterande chef, +4640331941, fredrik.liedberg@med.lu.se Sarah Quist, HR-partner, +4640391055, sarah.quist@med.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 11.Sep.2026 Last application date 26.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:953999/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral position in Solid State Physics",
      "description": "Postdoctoral position in Solid State Physics Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Postdoctoral position in Solid State Physics Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position is placed at the Division of Solid-State Physics at the Department of Physics, a division with around 100 employees where extensive research is carried out centered on various aspects of nanophysics, ranging from materials science to bio- and quantum physics to various applications. The division is a central part of NanoLund, Lund University's large interdisciplinary research center on nanoscience and nanotechnology. The research center includes also Lund Nano Lab, a central and shared cleanroom facility for the production of materials and components at the nanoscale. The Division has a strong commitment to undergraduate education, not least in the Master's programme \"Engineering Nanoscience\" at LTH. This position is part of a research group consisting of three doctoral students with individual projects where collaboration leads to good group development. Subject description The ZEUS project focuses on advancing the development of innovative and radiation-resistant nanowire solar cells with high efficiency. While current space-tested nanowire solar cells offer around 15% efficiency with single-bandgap cells, ZEUS aims to significantly improve this efficiency, potentially up to 47%, by using triple-junction nanowire cells using III-V semiconductor materials. To this end, this interdisciplinary project will also optimize the surface passivation of nanowires to improve voltage and current matching. The project aims to achieve scalability by transferring nanowire solar cells onto lightweight, flexible substrates. The main objectives include: Increasing the efficiency of radiation-resistant nanowire solar cells. Scaling up the substrate size to 100 mm^2 and developing modules with a size of 1x1 cm2. Reducing weight and material usage through nanowire peeling and substrate reuse. A short specific subject description can be used to describe and clarify the research area/s included in more detail. This description governs the selection process. Work duties The main duties involved in a post-doctoral position is to conduct research. Teaching may also be included, but up to no more than 20% of working hours. The position includes the opportunity for three weeks of training in higher education teaching and learning. The purpose of the position is to develop the independence as a researcher and to create the opportunity of further development. You will work on the synthesis of nanowires and their processing into solar cells for space applications. Characterization of the nanowires and their evaluation with respect of external quantum efficiency (EQE) and current-voltage characterization (IV) will be part of the project. Detailed description of the work duties: Epitaxy, cleanroom work Experimental evaluation of semiconductor samples, with respect to nanowires. Supervision of degree projects and doctoral students Administration related to the work duties listed above Qualification requirements Appointment to a post-doctoral position requires that the applicant has a PhD, or an international degree deemed equivalent to a PhD, within the subject of the position. The certificate proving the qualification requirement is met, must be received before the employment decision is made. Priority will be given to candidates who have graduated no more than three years ago before the last day for application. Under special circumstances, the doctoral degree can have been completed earlier. Additional requirements: Very good oral and written proficiency in English. You have documented experience of processing skills in clean room environment (ex. Lithography, metal evaporation, wet bench chemistry etc.) Practical experience using standard characterisation methods for evaluation of morphology and materials quality of nanowires. (XRD, SEM, AFM, µ-PL, CL, EBIC etc) You have proven collaborative skills and individual capacity. Assessment criteria This is a career development position primarily focused on research. The position is intended as an initial step in a career, and the assessment of the applicants will primarily be based on their research qualifications and potential as researchers. Particular emphasis will be placed on research skills within the subject. For appointments to a post-doctoral position, the following shall form the assessment criteria: A good ability to develop and conduct high quality research. Teaching skills. Other qualifications: You have proven skills of Epitaxy, MOVPE and nanowire synthesis. You have experience of electro optical characterisation of processed semiconductor samples (I-V, EQE, PL, analyses etc.). Consideration will also be given to how the applicant’s experience and skills complement and strengthen ongoing research within the department, and how they stand to contribute to its future development. We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. Read more on the University website about being a Lund University employee. Further information This is a full-time, fixed-term employment of 2 years. The period of employment is determined in accordance with the agreement “Avtal om tidsbegränsad anställning som postdoktor” (“Agreement on fixed-term employment as a post-doctoral fellow”). How to apply Applications are to be submitted via the University’s recruitment system. The application should include a CV and a personal letter justifying your interest in the position and how it matches your qualifications. The application should also include a degree certificate or equivalent and any other document to which you would like to draw attention (copies of grade transcripts, details of referees, letters of recommendation, etc.)” We welcome your application! About the Faculty of Engineering LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. The Department of Physics is with a staff of about 400 scientists and educators one of the largest departments within Lund University. There are seven research divisions and a number of research centra within the department. The research activities at the department cover a broad spectrum of modern physics. www.fysik.lu.se/english. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment Enligt överenskommelse Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2300 Contact Magnus Borgström, Professor, +46462221494, magnus.borgstrom@ftf.lth.se Union representative SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 07.Sep.2026 Last application date 28.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:967223/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral position in machine learning for the development of novel semiconductor materials",
      "description": "Postdoctoral position in machine learning for the development of novel semiconductor materials Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Postdoctoral position in machine learning for the development of novel semiconductor materials Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position is placed at the Division of Solid State Physics at the Department of Physics, a division with around 100 employees where extensive research is carried out centered on various aspects of nanophysics, ranging from materials science to bio- and quantum physics to various applications. The division is a central part of NanoLund, Lund University's large interdisciplinary research Centre on nanoscience and nanotechnology. The division hosts the research group of Prof Vanya Darakchieva whose research over the past twenty years on the fabrication and advanced characterization of large bandgap semiconductors has been at the international forefront. More information can be found at: www.ftf.lth.se , www.nano.lu.se , https://kaw.wallenberg.org/en/research/semiconductor-bandgap-key-future-green-electronics, https://c3nit.se/ Subject description The purpose of this project is to develop a data-driven approach for optimizing the growth of semiconductor materials by combining machine learning with a physics-based understanding of the growth process. Doping and processing of ultra-wide bandgap semiconductors present challenges, but it can enable electronic devices with substantially better performance as compared to traditional power electronic devices based on Si and SiC. The position is part of a large initiative funded by KAW to develop a comprehensive platform that will enable, for the first time, deterministic control of atomic configurations in UWBG materials. The project combines leading expertise and unique know-how in theory, modelling, synthesis, atomic-scale imaging and spectroscopy, and nanoelectronics aiming to unlock the potential of AlN and UWBG materials for next-generation energy technologies. The research work will be conducted in close collaboration with PhD-students and researchers working with power electronic devices within the competence center for III-Nitride technology C3NiT and within a European network within the WBG Pilot Line, but also with experienced researchers within materials growth and materials characterization, at the departments of solid state physics and synchrotron radiation physics. Work duties The main duties involved in a post-doctoral position is to conduct research. Teaching may also be included, but up to no more than 20% of working hours. The position includes the opportunity for three weeks of training in higher education teaching and learning. The purpose of the position is to develop the independence as a researcher and to create the opportunity of further development. The research tasks will include to develop machine-learning methods using experimental data provided by collaborating experimentalists. A central part of the work will be to identify and define the most relevant parameters governing material growth based on the underlying physics, and to develop an open-source computational framework for analyzing experimental data, identifying relationships between growth conditions and material properties, and proposing optimized growth parameters. Qualification requirements Appointment to a post-doctoral position requires that the applicant has a PhD, or an international degree deemed equivalent to a PhD, within the subject of the position. The certificate proving the qualification requirement is met, must be received before the employment decision is made. Priority will be given to candidates who have graduated no more than three years ago before the last day for application. Under special circumstances, the doctoral degree can have been completed earlier. Additional requirements: Very good oral and written proficiency in English. The subject area of the doctoral degree should be in physics, materials science or equivalent, specializing in machine learning and data-driven approaches Experience in modelling of multiphase systems (e.g. liquid/solid, gas/liquid, liquid/liquid, or gas/solid) Experience in application of machine learning approaches. Experience in scientific computing for experimental data analysis Assessment criteria This is a career development position primarily focused on research. The position is intended as an initial step in a career, and the assessment of the applicants will primarily be based on their research qualifications and potential as researchers. Particular emphasis will be placed on research skills within the subject. For appointments to a post-doctoral position, the following shall form the assessment criteria: A good ability to develop and conduct high-quality research. Teaching skills. Other qualifications: Knowledge of semiconductor physics. Knowledge of ultrawide bandgap semiconductors. Proven experience in writing scientific papers. Consideration will also be given to how the applicant’s experience and skills complement and strengthen ongoing research within the department, and how they stand to contribute to its future development. We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. You will be included in a team of researchers at NanoLund, a highly visible nanoscience center that offers excellent career development, an open and inclusive research environment with an active community of PhD students and postdocs, and access to a wide range of state-of-the-art capabilities within characterization, nanofabrication and modelling. Read more on the University website about being a Lund University employee. Further information This is a full-time, fixed-term employment of 3 years. The period of employment is determined in accordance with the agreement “Avtal om tidsbegränsad anställning som postdoktor” (“Agreement on fixed-term employment as a post-doctoral fellow”). How to apply Applications are to be submitted via the University’s recruitment system. The application should include a CV and a personal letter justifying your interest in the position and how it matches your qualifications. The application should also include a degree certificate or equivalent and any other document to which you would like to draw attention (copies of grade transcripts, details of referees, letters of recommendation, etc.)” We welcome your application! About the Faculty of Engineering LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. The Department of Physics is with a staff of about 400 scientists and educators one of the largest departments within Lund University. There are seven research divisions and a number of research centra within the department. The research activities at the department cover a broad spectrum of modern physics. www.fysik.lu.se/english. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment According to agreement Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2619 Contact Vanya Darakchieva, Professor, 046-2220000, vanya.darakchieva@ftf.lth.se Union representative OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 14.Sep.2026 Last application date 28.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:966346/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "PhD position in Neuroscience",
      "description": "PhD position in Neuroscience Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) PhD position in Neuroscience Login and apply Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Sciences, Lund Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. PhD student in Medical Science – Neuroscience, with a Focus on Stroke Care Organisation and Improved Diagnostics and Clinical Workflow in Acute Ischaemic Stroke. Description of the workplace The PhD project will be carried out within the field of neuroscience. The research group is led by Associate Professor Teresa Ullberg and conducts clinical research focusing on stroke, with particular emphasis on acute ischaemic stroke, diagnostics, and the organisation of stroke care. The group has a close and well-established collaboration with the Lund Stroke Imaging Group, led by Associate Professor Johan Wassélius. The research environment is interdisciplinary and based on close collaboration between neurology and radiology, with a particular focus on the development and evaluation of diagnostic methods and clinical workflows in stroke care. The combined research groups include approximately ten doctoral students and offer a well-established infrastructure for doctoral education, supervision, research meetings, and seminars. The doctoral student will become part of an active and multidisciplinary research environment with access to experienced supervisors and regular seminar and research activities. The Department of Clinical Sciences, Lund, is now announcing a PhD student position within the doctoral programme subject Medical Science – Neuroscience, linked to the doctoral project “Incidence, Treatment, Radiological and Organisational Aspects of Acute Ischaemic Stroke with Large Vessel Occlusion” . The position is based at the Faculty of Medicine, Lund University, and is conducted within an international and interdisciplinary environment. We offer Lund University is a government agency, which means that you will receive particular benefits, generous annual leave, and a favourable occupational pension scheme. We also offer flexible working hours, creating excellent conditions for a healthy work-life balance. Read more about working at Lund University on our website. Work duties and areas of responsibility As a PhD student, your primary task will be to pursue doctoral studies through independent research under supervision within the project “Incidence, Treatment, Radiological and Organisational Aspects of Acute Ischaemic Stroke with Large Vessel Occlusion” . The research focuses on the organisation of stroke healthcare, as well as improved diagnostics and clinical workflow in acute ischaemic stroke. The project will be conducted through a series of clinical research studies and is closely linked to highly specialised stroke care and neurointerventional services. The PhD student will analyze and process large research datasets, interpret and evaluate scientific results, and contribute to the writing and publication of scientific manuscripts as part of the doctoral education. The position also provides opportunities to actively participate in national and international scientific conferences and to present research findings. Within the PhD programme, the doctoral student will have the opportunity to complete all mandatory doctoral courses as well as relevant advanced courses within the subject area and in scientific methodology. This will provide excellent opportunities to develop both subject-specific expertise and methodological competence, as well as independence in scientific work. The PhD's main task is to undertake doctoral education through independent research under supervision within the project “Incidence, Treatment, Radiological and Organisational Aspects of Acute Ischaemic Stroke with Large Vessel Occlusion” . The doctoral student will analyse and process large amounts of research data, interpret and evaluate findings, and contribute to the preparation and publication of scientific manuscripts. Qualifications Requirements For employment as a doctoral student, the applicant must have: A medical degree or another relevant academic qualification in medicine or a related biomedical field, alternatively be enrolled in a medical degree programme. Excellent command of Swedish and English, both spoken and written. Good knowledge of data analysis and statistics, including experience working with large datasets. The ability, under supervision, to structure, process and analyse research data and to document and present scientific findings. A meticulous, analytical, and responsible approach to work. Meritorious qualifications The following qualifications are considered advantageous: Completed courses in epidemiology, biostatistics, research methodology, or equivalent. Education or experience in medicine with a focus on neurology, neuroradiology, or stroke. Research experience, particularly in clinical and registry-based research. Experience working with quality registries and large-scale datasets (big data). Experience in statistical analysis and the use of relevant statistical software. Experience in medical imaging and basic assessment of brain computed tomography (CT) scans. Strong personal qualities and demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively within a research group. Eligibility Applicants must fulfil the general eligibility requirements for doctoral education, meaning: a second-cycle qualification (Master's degree or equivalent), or completed course requirements of at least 240 higher education credits, including at least 60 credits at second-cycle level, or acquired substantially equivalent knowledge in another way. Assessment criteria Among eligible applicants, particular emphasis will be placed on: Scientific maturity and analytical ability. Motivation, independence, and collaborative skills. Ability to benefit from doctoral education. The extent to which the applicant’s background and experience are considered capable of contributing to the successful implementation of the project. Terms of employment The employment is a fixed-term full-time position as a PhD student for four years (48 months), in accordance with Chapter 5, Section 7 of the Swedish Higher Education Ordinance. The planned starting date is 1 November 2026, or as otherwise agreed. The Department of Clinical Sciences Lund (IKVL) is one of six departments within the Faculty of Medicine. The department maintains extensive collaborations both within the faculty and with other units of Lund University. IKVL also has a well-developed collaboration with Skåne University Hospital (SUS), Region Skåne, and external stakeholders in society and industry. These collaborations aim to promote clinical and experimental translational research, development, and education. The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Employment expires 2030-10-31 First day of employment 2026-11-01 Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2591 Contact Teresa Ullberg, docent, 072-7434402, teresa.ullberg@med.lu.se Jessica Hörngren, HR partner, 073-6361904, jessica.horngren@med.lu.se Union representative OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362 SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366 Published 08.Sep.2026 Last application date 29.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:964381/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "PhD student in Medical Science, Gerontology, in Medical and Health Sciences",
      "description": "PhD student in Medical Science, Gerontology, in Medical and Health Sciences Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) PhD student in Medical Science, Gerontology, in Medical and Health Sciences Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The Department of Health Sciences, Faculty of Medicine, announces a position as a PhD-student with an accompanying employment within the Ageing and Health research group. The research group conducts interdisciplinary research with a focus on ageing and health. The research group is affiliated with the Centre for Ageing and Supportive Environments (CASE), responsible for the coordination of the Swedish National Graduate School on Ageing and Health (SWEAH) and engaged in the university’s profile area Proactive Ageing. Further information about the group can be found here . We hereby invite applications for a doctoral position in gerontology, specializing in medical and health sciences. The appointment is full-time (100%). The desired start date is 1 november 2026, or as agreed. Candidates invited for an interview may be asked to complete a work test to demonstrate basic competence for the position. Description of the project AI is rapidly transforming home care for older adults. Automation tools such as social robots, chatbots, sensor systems, and automated documentation and scheduling promise to increase efficiency, support independent living and relieve pressure on care systems. At the same time, they may change what counts as “care”, who is included or excluded from care, how risks and responsibilities are distributed among older adults, care workers, organisations and technology providers, as well as may shape social inequalities and human vulnerabilities. The position is part of the Nordic-Baltic collaborative project entitled “AI for Mindful Care and Ageing” (AMICA), which explores how vulnerability is constructed, negotiated and practiced in AI-automated home care for older adults. The overall aims of the project are to: (1) generate empirical knowledge on how AI automation transforms experiences of care, care labor, inequality and welfare systems, (2) advance theoretical understanding of how vulnerability is shaped by intersecting social identities, life-course histories and structural conditions, and (3) inform policy and governance for responsible and ethical AI in home care in the Nordic–Baltic region. The project combines gerontology with critical social science perspectives, and science and technology studies. The student will focus on AI-enabled home care in Sweden and conduct cross-country comparisons in collaboration with researchers from partner universities. Work duties and responsibilities The main task of doctoral students is to devote themselves to their doctoral education; you will be both admitted as a doctoral student and employed at Lund University. The doctoral education includes completion of the requirements specified in the General Study Plan for Doctoral Studies in Medical Science. With support from the main and co-supervisors, the PhD student is expected to: plan the PhD project, collect data, analyse and compile data and report the results in scientific articles in English summarize the doctoral student project in conclusion in a framework and present in a doctoral dissertation take the necessary courses at doctoral level, actively participate in seminars and other relevant activities and familiarize with literature relevant to the research area actively participate in communication and translation of research results to various target groups, both within and outside the research community travel both domestically and internationally participate in and contribute to the research environment in the research group, at the department, within CASE and the profile area Proactive Ageing The work duties might also include teaching and other departmental duties (no more than 20%). Admission requirements A person meets the general entry requirements for the doctoral program if he or she: has been awarded a second-cycle qualification, or has met the requirements for courses comprising at least 240 credits of which at least 60 credits were awarded in the second cycle, or has acquired substantially equivalent knowledge in some other way in Sweden or abroad. In addition, there is a requirement for sufficient knowledge within the subject area for the doctoral program. A person meets the specific admission requirements for third-cycle studies in medical science if he or she has at least 60 credits within the subject area of medical science. Students with a first-cycle qualification obtained outside Scandinavia are to have knowledge equivalent to 3 years of studying English in upper secondary school (a pass in the exam) if they have a first language other than English. Additional requirements good proficiency in English, spoken and written good proficiency in Swedish, spoken and written good computer skills Meritorious for the position are: demonstrated competence in, or methodological orientation toward, qualitative research methods and theory development experience of collaboration in multidisciplinary and/or transnational research teams experience working with or conducting research with older adults experience in research or development projects involving digital technologies What we offer Lund University is a government agency, which means you receive special benefits, generous holiday allowance and a favourable occupational pension. Read more on the university’s website, Work with us . About the employment The position is a full-time, fixed-term appointment, with a desired start date of 01/11/2026, or as agreed. Doctoral studies are four years when studying full-time. If you take on teaching or other departmental duties, your employment will be extended accordingly. Provisions regarding employment as a doctoral student can be found in the Higher Education Ordinance (1993:100), Chapter 5, Sections 1–7. More about terms of employment available on the staff pages . How to apply The position is sought via the university’s recruitment system. Applications should include a personal motivation letter (in English), in which you describe how you meet the qualification requirements and why you are interested in the position. The application should also contain relevant diploma, CV or equivalent, contact information for two reference persons, and a writing (essay) sample of no more than 10 pages. The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. At the Department of Health Sciences, we work to increase knowledge about how people can recover and maintain their health. We train all categories of future healthcare and rehabilitation staff within first, second and third cycle education. Our research focuses on aspects of health: prevention, therapy, healthcare, social care and rehabilitation. Our activities in Malmö and Lund are closely linked to healthcare at Skåne University Hospital and primary and municipal healthcare. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Employment expires 0001-11-30 First day of employment Desired date 261101 or as agreed Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2520 Contact Wenqien Xu, handledare, wenqian.xu@med.lu.se Evelina Petersson, HR-partner, +46462221920, evelina.petersson@med.lu.se Susanne Iwarsson, forskargruppchef, +46462221940, susanne.iwarsson@med.lu.se Union representative OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, kansli@saco-s.lu.se, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 09.Sep.2026 Last application date 29.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:965334/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "PhD student in Chromatin & Genome Integrity",
      "description": "PhD student in Chromatin & Genome Integrity Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) PhD student in Chromatin & Genome Integrity Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The Chromatin & Genome Integrity Lab (Stem Cell Center & Department of Experimental Medical Science, Faculty of Medicine), led by Dr. Giulia Saredi, studies the mechanisms that regulate chromatin structure and genome stability in human cells, and how these processes safeguard human health. The lab fosters a respectful and inclusive environment, with a strong emphasis on open communication and scientific curiosity. We believe that impactful research is best achieved through collaboration and the exchange of ideas; the successful candidate will be encouraged to actively engage in the group’s scientific activities and to develop their skills in a supportive research setting. Project description This PhD project will investigate how chromatin structure is maintained during the cellular response to DNA damage and how these processes safeguard genome and epigenome integrity. The successful candidate will combine cell biology, biochemistry, high-content imaging, and state-of-the-art genomic and epigenomic approaches to study chromatin dynamics in human cells and stem cell-derived neuronal models. The project will generate fundamental insights into the chromatin-based mechanisms that preserve (epi)genome integrity and prevent human disease. Tasks and responsibilities The main task for a doctoral student is to devote yourself to your doctoral studies, therefore you will be both admitted as a student and employed at Lund University. The successful candidate is expected to lead their research project under the supervision of the group leader and to actively engage in the intellectual and practical activities of the research group, including lab meetings, scientific discussions, and laboratory organization. Qualifications Requirements for the position are: A second-cycle degree, completed course requirements of at least 240 higher education credits, of which at least 60 higher education credits at the second-cycle level, or in some other way acquired essentially equivalent knowledge within or outside the country. Good ability to utilize the doctoral studies You have expertise in studying chromatin biology and DNA repair mechanisms in human cells. You have experience in human cell culture techniques as well as knowledge of human iPSCs culturing. You have expertise in molecular cloning, western blotting, and quantitative image-based microscopy. You have experience in performing assays to study genome stability and to determine cell survival in response to genotoxic drug treatment. You have a high level of fluency in written and spoken English. You have a high level of self-motivation and the ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a team. Great emphasis will be placed on how the applicant, through his/her experience and competence, is judged to have the ability needed to complete the doctoral studies What we offer Lund University is a government agency, which means that you get special benefits, generous holidays and a favourable occupational pension.  As a doctoral student at Lund University, you are both admitted as a student and employed, which gives you several benefits. Read more about being admitted and employed at the university's website. Find out more on the University website about working with us Work for us About the employment The employment is a fixed-term employment for a total of four years, 100% with start date on 1st January 2027 or as agreed. The main rule is that employment at Lund University is conducted in Sweden, you are therefore expected to be able to perform your work on site in Lund. Rules and regulations concerning employment as a doctoral student can be found in the Higher Education Ordinance (1993:100), Chapter 5, Sections 1-7. Provisions regarding admission to doctoral studies are found in Chapter 6, Section 3 of the Higher Education Ordinance (1993:100). More information is available on the university's website Other information Interviews will be conducted online shortly after the application deadline. How to apply You apply for the position via the university's recruitment system. The application should include a personal letter which explains why you are interested in the position, how the position matches your qualifications, as well as the names and contacts of two references. The application should also include your degree certificate and your CV. The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2553 Published 14.Sep.2026 Last application date 04.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:968749/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Project assistant in infrastructure transitions and economic geography",
      "description": "Project assistant in infrastructure transitions and economic geography Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Project assistant in infrastructure transitions and economic geography Login and apply Lunds universitet Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position is based at the Department of Human Geography. You will work in the research project CATS (Beyond herding cats: integrating research on infrastructure transitions across project boundaries), funded by the Crafoord Foundation and running 2026–2027. CATS connects several ongoing research projects on how infrastructure for water, sanitation, food, mobility and waste is becoming more decentralised, with researchers at Lund University and international partners. The project facilitates exchange and communicates the results to researchers, policymakers and the wider public. You will work closely with the project leader and be based at Geocentrum I in Lund, together with the department's roughly 60 staff. You will also be affiliated with CIRCLE, the Centre for Innovation Research at Lund University (www.circle.lu.se), an interdisciplinary research environment with a large national and international network. What we offer Lund University is a public authority, which means that you receive particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension. We also have a flexitime agreement that creates good conditions for work-life balance. You will be part of an international research environment with seminars, workshops and networks at both the department and CIRCLE. Read more on the University website about being an employee at Lund University: https://www.lunduniversity.lu.se/about-university/work-us Work duties and areas of responsibility As project assistant you coordinate the day-to-day work of CATS and support the project leader and the research group. The tasks are partly administrative, partly research-related. Administration and communication plan and run workshops, seminars and other events, including travel bookings and practical arrangements update the website and social media, and produce newsletters assist with material for financial follow-up and reporting ongoing project administration, such as meeting notes, document management and contact with partners Research support literature searches and overviews of the state of research collecting, sorting, transcribing and documenting data, such as interviews and documents analysis of qualitative and/or quantitative material contributing to reports, presentations, applications and other texts The balance between tasks varies over time and is adapted to the needs of the project and your experience. The work requires regular presence at the department in Lund. Some travel within Sweden and Europe may occur. Qualifications Requirements for the position are: You have a bachelor's degree in the social sciences or another field relevant to the position, or are in the final stage of such a degree. You have good knowledge of English, spoken and written. You have experience of searching and compiling literature and of working with qualitative or quantitative material, for example from thesis work. You have good analytical skills. You are well organised and independent, and able to plan and prioritise your work. You have good collaboration skills. Additional merits for the position are: You hold or are studying towards a master's degree in human geography, economic geography, innovation studies, political science, sociology or a related subject. You have experience of administrative work, such as organising events, working with websites and social media, or preparing simple financial summaries. You are interested in research on sustainability transitions or infrastructure. You have good knowledge of Swedish. Other The employment is a fixed-term employment (special fixed-term employment) for one year at 50–100 per cent, by agreement. Start as soon as possible or as agreed. Interviews will be held in Lund or online shortly after the application deadline. Contact Johan Miörner (johan.miorner@keg.lu.se) for questions regarding the position. How to apply Applications are submitted via the University's recruitment system. The application must be written in English and contain the following: a cover letter (max. 1 page) describing why you are applying, how your experience matches the tasks and what extent (50–100 per cent) you are able to work CV degree certificate, transcript of records or equivalent contact details for two references You may also attach transcripts, work samples or other documents you wish to cite. The University applies individual salary setting. Please state your salary expectations in your application. The Faculty of Social Sciences at Lund University is one of the leading education and research institutions in Sweden and operates both in Lund and Helsingborg. The Faculty of Social Sciences at Lund University is one of the leading education and research institutions in Sweden and operates both in Lund and Helsingborg. The Department of Human Geography (Institutionen för kulturgeografi och ekonomisk geografi) at Lund University is a research-intensive academic unit within the Faculty of Social Sciences. It combines long academic traditions with modern and highly internationalised research and education. The department hosts a broad portfolio of education at undergraduate, master’s and doctoral levels, including internationally oriented programmes such as the Master’s in Human Geography. The department comprises a diverse faculty. The exact staffing varies, but the department currently employs 55–60 academic and administrative staff. The department educates roughly 400 full-time students across bachelor’s and master’s programmes, and it employs about 15 doctoral students. The research environment is strongly oriented towards external funding and has been successful competitively at national and European levels. The department’s research and education activities have received very favourable assessments in internal and external evaluations, positioning it among Lund University’s leading disciplinary domains. Lund University as a whole performs prominently in global rankings. According to the most recent QS World University Rankings, Lund University is placed #72 globally and achieves strong subject-level positions, including in fields related to human geography and environmental sciences. Lund University also ranks #1 in the world for sustainability in the same ranking, reflecting its strengths in socially and environmentally relevant research and education. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Special fixed-term employment Contract type Full-time/Part-time First day of employment Tillträde snarast eller enligt överenskommelse Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 50-100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2665 Contact Johan Miörner, 0462220000, johan.miorner@keg.lu.se Elin Johansson, 046222000, elin.johansson@sam.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 14.Sep.2026 Last application date 05.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:953827/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Physics: Topoelectronic Quantum Devices in 2D Materials",
      "description": "Doctoral student in Physics: Topoelectronic Quantum Devices in 2D Materials Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Doctoral student in Physics: Topoelectronic Quantum Devices in 2D Materials Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position is placed at the Division of Solid-State Physics at the Department of Physics, a division with around 100 employees where extensive research is carried out centered on various aspects of nanophysics, ranging from materials science to bio- and quantum physics to various applications. The division is a central part of NanoLund, Lund University's large interdisciplinary research center on nanoscience and nanotechnology. The research center includes also Lund Nano Lab, a central and shared cleanroom facility for the production of materials and components at the nanoscale. The Division has a strong commitment to undergraduate education, not least in the Master's programme \"Engineering Nanoscience\" at LTH. This position is part of a research group consisting of three doctoral students with individual projects where collaboration leads to good group development. You will join a research group that conducts experimental research in quantum topoelectronics. We aim to develop novel quantum devices, including qubits, by harnessing electronic correlations and topology in two-dimensional materials. We offer a friendly and collaborative research environment that encourages creative thinking and ambitious research beyond the current state of the art. Being a doctoral student As a doctoral student, you are both admitted as a student and employed at Lund University. As a doctoral student, you will be trained in a scientific approach. In short, you will be trained to think critically and analytically, to solve problems independently using the right methods, and to develop an awareness of research ethics. In addition, you will have the opportunity to work on projects, to develop your leadership and pedagogical skills. Throughout your studies, you will be guided by supervisors. Doctoral studies end with a thesis and a doctoral degree. More about being a doctoral student at LTH on lth.se. Subject and project description This PhD project focuses on the DC characterization and microwave-frequency investigation of anyonic excitations in graphene-based quantum devices. The doctoral student will fabricate ultrahigh-quality van der Waals heterostructures in Lund Nano Lab and study them using low-temperature electronic and microwave measurements. The student will also contribute to establishing a new experimental laboratory, including infrastructure for van der Waals device fabrication and the installation of a dilution refrigerator reaching temperatures below 10 mK, equipped with a 14 T magnet and both DC and RF measurement lines. The position provides extensive hands-on experience in nanofabrication, cryogenic measurement techniques, microwave electronics, data analysis, and the physics of quantum devices. The student will be an important member of a collaborative research team and will work with research groups at Lund University and leading international institutions. The project combines fundamental quantum physics with a technology-oriented approach to developing new types of nanoelectronic and quantum devices. Work duties You will primarily devote yourself to your doctoral programme, which includes participation in research projects as well as third cycle courses, seminars and conferences. The purpose of the PhD project is to build anyons arrays and to operate them as qubits. You will work independently as well as in close collaboration with the other partners in the project. The work duties include: Device fabrication in cleanroom environment in Lund Nano Lab. Transport and high-frequency microwave measurements, including programming of measurement scripts. Analysis of the measurement results to gain understanding on their operation and to design new devices. Writing scientific publications on the results and presenting them at conferences. Using, servicing, and developing equipment in the measurement labs, including the installation of a dilution refrigerator. The duties also include participation in teaching and other departmental work (however, a maximum of 20% of working hours). Qualifications To be eligible for admission and employment as a doctoral student, you must fulfil the requirements below. Admission requirements A person meets the general admission requirements for third-cycle courses and study programmes if the applicant: has been awarded a second-cycle qualification, or has satisfied the requirements for courses comprising at least 240 credits of which at least 60 credits were awarded in the second cycle, or has acquired substantially equivalent knowledge in some other way in Sweden or abroad. A person meets the specific admission requirements for third-cycle programme in Physics, if they: completed at least 45 second-cycle credits in physics, engineering physics or an associated field, or has a second cycle degree in physics, engineering physics or an associated field. Additional requirements In order to complete the doctoral programme in question, the following are also required: good knowledge of quantum physics. good ability to work independently and to formulate and tackle research problems. good written and oral communication skills. good ability to cooperate. very good knowledge of English, spoken and written. Other qualifications (advantages) For this doctoral position, particular consideration will be given to candidates with: Knowledge and experience on quantum device. Experience on electrical measurement. Experience on cryogenics. Knowledge and experience of nanofabrication processes. Experience in van der walls stacking processes. Ability to think creatively and analytically. Ability to do skillful laboratory work. We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. Our group and research environment offers further a creative and vibrant environment to do top-notch scientific research with state-of-the-art equipment. This allows you to gain experience in many different types of work tasks, and a unique opportunity to develop as a researcher. More about working at Lund University on lu.se. About the employment The employment is a fixed-term employment at full time, starting as agreed, preferrably starting from January 2027. Third cycle studies at LTH consist of full-time studies for 4 years. In the case of teaching and other departmental duties, the employment is extended accordingly. Doctoral studentships are regulated in the Higher Education Ordinance (1993:100), chapter 5, 1-7 §§. More about terms of employment for doctoral students on Lund University’s Staffpages. How to apply Applications shall be written in English and include: CV and a cover letter stating the reasons why you are interested in the doctoral programme/employment and in what way the research project corresponds to your interests and educational background. Copies of issued study certificates and/or awarded degree certificates. These must confirm that you meet the general and specific admission requirements for the doctoral programme and show that you have the subject knowledge required for the doctoral programme project. Contact details of at least one reference. We welcome your application. About the Faculty of Engineering LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. The Department of Physics is with a staff of about 400 scientists and educators one of the largest departments within Lund University. There are seven research divisions and a number of research centra within the department. The research activities at the department cover a broad spectrum of modern physics. www.fysik.lu.se/english. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position First day of employment Enligt överenskommelse Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2292 Contact Marco Valentini, Biträdande universitetslektor, +18055684667, marco.valentini@fysik.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 10.Sep.2026 Last application date 09.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:967435/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral position in COMPEL-Energy Sciences with a focus on thermal runaway modeling",
      "description": "Postdoctoral position in COMPEL-Energy Sciences with a focus on thermal runaway modeling Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Postdoctoral position in COMPEL-Energy Sciences with a focus on thermal runaway modeling Login and apply Lund University, Faculty of Engeneering, Department of Energy Sciences Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position is located at the Division of Heat Transfer at the Department of Energy Sciences. At the division, we conduct research and education on the energy generation, conversion, and utilization in different electro- and thermo-mechanical systems where the study of heat transfer, fluid mechanics, reactive flows, as well as solid mechanics plays a central role. In particular, improved understanding of turbulent, reactive, and multiphase flows together with material sciences and novel manufacturing techniques is the key to improved heat transfer mechanisms and more efficient use of energy. Approximately 25 colleagues work at the division, including 15 PhD students. On the international level we collaborate with universities and institutes in Europe, Asia, and North America. More information about the Division of Heat Transfer can be found here: https://www.ht.energy.lth.se/english/ COMPEL, \"COMPetence for the ELectrification of the transport system\", is a strategic initiative by the Swedish government, aimed at strengthening research and educational environments within battery technology and electrification. Within COMPEL, the three institutions - Chalmers University of Technology, Lund University, and Uppsala University – in consultation with Swedish industry, deepen their collaboration with the aim of strengthening Sweden's position in battery technology contributing to the electrification of the transport sector, and related areas. The three institutions aim to enhance research and education in the battery sector to ensure strong research in battery technology supporting that electrification of the transport sector is maintained and further developed in Sweden. Research within COMPEL spans the entire battery value chain: materials research, research on new cell concepts and cell manufacturing, battery systems and system integration, recycling, and safety. At Lund University with the Faculty of Engineering (LTH), COMPEL is a strategic initiative aimed at enhancing research environments in battery technology and electrification, in a manner that complements relevant research at other institutions and builds on our strengths. COMPEL at Lund University initially includes researchers from ten departments, and three areas of research have been identified for strategic development: materials research, integration of batteries with power electronics, and safe energy storage. On the educational side, the number of students in several of the Master programs is being increased, and the course offerings related to battery technology and electrification are being strengthened. This environment is a smorgasbord of resources and opportunities for those who wants to develop their careers and make a difference in the transition towards a sustainable future. Subject description This project focuses on the development of GPU-accelerated, high-fidelity thermal runaway simulation models for lithium-ion battery cells, modules, packs, and complete battery systems. Thermal runaway is a chain reaction that leads to the uncontrolled release of energy stored within a battery cell, potentially resulting in fires and other hazardous events that are difficult to mitigate. As battery systems grow in size and complexity, there is an increasing need for computationally efficient predictive tools capable of resolving the underlying physical and chemical processes across multiple scales. The project will be undertaken in collaboration with Scania Industrial Battery Systems and the partners of the COMPEL initiative. The primary goal of the postdoctoral project is to develop next-generation GPU-based simulation methodologies in OpenFOAM for predicting thermal runaway initiation and propagation at the cell- and module level. Particular emphasis will be placed on leveraging modern heterogeneous computing architectures to enable large-scale, high-fidelity reacting-flow simulations. The models developed at the cell level will subsequently be coupled to module-, pack-, and system-level simulations to improve the prediction of thermal runaway propagation in practical battery systems. Work duties The main duties involved in a post-doctoral position is to conduct research. Teaching may also be included, but up to no more than 20% of working hours. The position includes the opportunity for three weeks of training in higher education teaching and learning. The purpose of the position is to develop the independence as a researcher and to create the opportunity of further development. Detailed description of the work duties, such as: Develop and implement GPU-accelerated thermal runaway models and numerical methods in OpenFOAM. Design and optimize computational frameworks for high-performance simulation of reacting flows and battery safety phenomena on modern GPU architectures. Conduct research in related areas of battery modeling, computational fluid dynamics (CFD), and high-performance computing (HPC). Analyze simulation results and publish findings in leading scientific journals and international conferences. Collaborate with industrial and academic partners within Scania Industrial Battery Systems and the COMPEL initiative. Interact with the research team to develop innovative methodologies in battery safety, CFD, and GPU computing. Contribute to applications for external research funding and participate in the development of future research initiatives. Participate in teaching activities, including supervision of doctoral student and master's degree projects. Qualification requirements Appointment to a post-doctoral position requires that the applicant has a PhD, or an international degree deemed equivalent to a PhD, within the subject of the position. The certificate proving the qualification requirement is met, must be received before the employment decision is made. Priority will be given to candidates who have graduated no more than three years ago before the last day for application. Under special circumstances, the doctoral degree can have been completed earlier. Additional requirements: Very good oral and written proficiency in English. Documented experience of programing and/or energy system modelling Assessment criteria This is a career development position primarily focused on research. The position is intended as an initial step in a career, and the assessment of the applicants will primarily be based on their research qualifications and potential as researchers. Particular emphasis will be placed on research skills within the subject. For appointments to a post-doctoral position, the following shall form the assessment criteria: A good ability to develop and conduct high quality research. Teaching skills. Other qualifications: Documented experience in the development and optimization of scientific software for GPU-based computing platforms. Experience with programming using CUDA, HIP, SYCL, OpenACC, or equivalent technologies. Knowledge of porting CPU-based CFD and multiphysics codes to heterogeneous HPC architectures. Experience in performance analysis, profiling, and optimization of parallel applications using tools such as NVIDIA Nsight, rocProfiler, or similar. Strong understanding of parallel programming with MPI, OpenMP, and GPU-based programming models. Experience working on national or international supercomputing facilities. Knowledge of numerical methods for large-scale simulations of fluid flow, heat transfer, and chemically reacting systems. Documented ability to work effectively in interdisciplinary research projects. Teaching experience. Is independent and has a good ability to plan and deliver The ability to collaborate with external partners, for instance industry Education that constitutes an additional qualification Other that offers value to the position Consideration will also be given to how the applicant’s experience and skills complement and strengthen ongoing research within the department, and how they stand to contribute to its future development. We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. It is advisable to enter information on what your unit specifically can offer as a workplace. Read more on the University website about being a Lund University employee Work at Lund University . Further information This is a full-time, fixed-term employment of 2 years. The period of employment is determined in accordance with the agreement “Avtal om tidsbegränsad anställning som postdoktor” (“Agreement on fixed-term employment as a post-doctoral fellow”). How to apply Applications are to be submitted via the University’s recruitment system. The application should include a CV and a personal letter justifying your interest in the position and how it matches your qualifications. The application should also include a degree certificate or equivalent and any other document to which you would like to draw attention (copies of grade transcripts, details of referees, letters of recommendation, etc.)” Welcome to apply! LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment By agreement Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100% City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2624 Contact Martin Andersson, +46462224908, martin.andersson@energy.lth.se Christer Fureby, +46462224813, christer.fureby@energy.lth.se Union representative SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se Published 08.Sep.2026 Last application date 11.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:965115/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Applied Electromagnetics Engineer/Physicist",
      "description": "Applied Electromagnetics Engineer/Physicist Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Applied Electromagnetics Engineer/Physicist Login and apply Lund University, MAX IV, Accelerator development Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. MAX IV operates two storage rings and a linear accelerator to produce synchrotron radiation. Its largest accelerator, the 3 GeV electron storage ring, was the first in the world to use a multi-bend-achromat lattice, enabling ultrahigh-brightness light with extremely low emittance. Only a few such next-generation storage rings exist today, though many facilities worldwide are now upgrading in this direction. To stay at the forefront, MAX IV is designing a major upgrade of the 3 GeV ring, called MAX 4 U , aiming to deliver outstanding improvements in lightsource performance. This includes the development of innovative magnet and RF systems. MAX IV is looking for an Electromagnetic Systems Engineer/Physicist to contribute to the development of new electromagnetic systems for MAX IV and MAX 4 U , with the opportunity to gradually take on increased technical responsibility for key accelerator components. Your team You will be working in the Accelerator Development group consisting of a wide variety of experts within the accelerator field, including accelerator physicists, high –frequency structure and magnet experts, and laser experts. The Accelerator Development team is a part of MAX IV Accelerator Division, which is responsible for developing and operating all accelerator-based light sources at MAX IV. This division is also charged with carrying out the major accelerator upgrade in the MAX 4 U project. About the role Electromagnetic systems are essential to the performance and development of the MAX IV accelerators. Their reliability and precision are key to stable operation and high-quality light delivery to our users. As part of MAX 4 U , several advanced accelerator systems will be developed and commissioned. We are therefore looking for an Electromagnetic Systems Engineer/Physicist who will play a key role in these developments while strengthening MAX IV’s in-house expertise and supporting our existing accelerator systems. Would you like to help develop the next generation of accelerator technology at one of the brightest light sources in the world? Your main work tasks will be Design, development, testing, and commissioning of advanced electromagnetic systems for MAX IV and MAX 4 U , such as: RF electron guns DC and AC magnets RF-based structures Contribute to the technical responsibility for electromagnetic accelerator subsystems. Preserve and strengthen in-house expertise in electromagnetic systems. Support the operation of the MAX IV accelerator systems. To be successful in this role you need to have the following qualifications University degree in Engineering or Physics or equivalent. Fluent in written and spoken English. Below are seen as merits Experience with electromagnetic design and simulation tools such as CST studio, COMSOL, GdfidL or FEMM. Previous experience in design of electromagnetic systems for particle accelerators such as: RF electron guns DC and AC magnets RF based structures Project managements skills. Experience working with particle accelerators. As a person you are collaborative and comfortable working as part of a team, while also being able to work independently and take initiative. You are curious, eager to learn, and have a genuine interest in technology. What we offer When you join our MAX IV team, you step into a world of front edge science. We make the invisible visible by supporting scientist from all over the world, generating scientific results for the benefit of society. We offer you a multicultural work environment with great opportunities for personal development with respect for a healthy work-life balance. Would you like to work in a challenging and supporting environment? Then join us and take the opportunity to make a real difference! For further information, please visit: https://www.lunduniversity.lu.se/about-lund-university/work-lund-university https://www.sweden.se https://www.maxiv.lu.se/about-us/careers/compensation-benefits/ Probationary period may apply. On-call services may apply. Lund University welcomes applicants with diverse backgrounds and experiences. We regard gender equality and diversity as a strength and an asset. MAX IV is a Swedish national large-scale research infrastructure hosted by Lund University. It provides scientists from Sweden and abroad, with intense x-rays and state-of-the-art instrumentation for research in areas such as engineering, physics, structural biology, chemistry and nanotechnology. Its 16 beamlines receive up to 2 000 scientists annually, conducting ground-breaking experiments in materials and life sciences using the brilliant X-rays. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Permanent position Contract type Full time First day of employment As per agreement Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2543 Contact Sara Thorin, +46708323395, sara.thorin@maxiv.lu.se Union representative OFR/S:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds, 046-2229364, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 14.Sep.2026 Last application date 12.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:961545/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Doctoral Student in Automatic Control with focus on Datacenter Control",
      "description": "Doctoral Student in Automatic Control with focus on Datacenter Control Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Doctoral Student in Automatic Control with focus on Datacenter Control Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Doctoral student in Automatic Control with focus on Datacenter Control Lund University, The Faculty of Engineering, Department Description of the workplace Automatic Control is an exciting and broad subject, covering both advanced mathematics and hands-on engineering. Historically, it has been instrumental in many areas, from the early development of telecommunications to the space race. Today, driven by the need to engineer ever larger systems, Automatic Control is more relevant than ever. The department of Automatic control has a stimulating and international environment with PhD students, postdocs, and teachers coming from all corners of the world. Research and teaching are conducted in an open and progressive atmosphere with collaborations both within academia and with industry, nationally as well as internationally. A PhD degree from the Department of Automatic control gives you the perfect base for an exciting career, be it in academia or industry. Lund University and the Department of Automatic Control welcome applicants with diverse backgrounds and experiences. We regard equality and diversity as a strength and an asset. For more information on being a doctoral student at the department, please have a look at our research videos: Research | Department of Automatic Control (lth.se). Being a doctoral student As a doctoral student, you are both admitted as a student and employed at Lund University. As a doctoral student, you will be trained in a scientific approach. In short, you will be trained to think critically and analytically, to solve problems independently using the right methods, and to develop an awareness of research ethics. In addition, you will have the opportunity to work on projects, to develop your leadership and pedagogical skills. Throughout your studies, you will be guided by supervisors. Doctoral studies end with a thesis and a doctoral degree. More about being a doctoral student at LTH on lth.se. https://www.lth.se/english/study-at-lth/phd-studies/ AEGIS — Adaptive Grid-Interactive Edge Datacenter Fleets is a European Marie Skłodowska-Curie Action (MSCA) research and training network. It is funded by the EU and trains 15 PhD students from 2026 to 2030. The main host for this position is Department of Automatic Control at Lund University. As part of the program you will spend two 3-months visits with partners in the network: École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne (Switzerland) and Elisa (Finland). Marie Skłodowska-Curie fellowships are among the most respected PhD positions in Europe. The position is only open to applicants that have not lived, studied, or worked in Sweden for more than 12 months in the 36 months before applying. More information about the project is available at the website https://aegis-dn.eu Subject and project description The AEGIS project sits where two fast-growing fields meet: datacentres and AI infrastructure on one side, energy and power systems on the other. Few people today can work across both, so the skills you build here are scarce and in high demand. Specifically there is a gap between a high-level goal like “cut carbon now” and the low-level controls of a site’s cooling, batteries, and compute hardware. In this project you close that gap. You will build a control layer that takes “intents” and translates them into a coordinated set of actions across the site’s physical assets. The models close the loop and tune themselves online using both classical control, AI, and reinforcement learning. In the project you will work on: Designing an intent language that captures carbon, flexibility, and cost. Breaking high-level intents down into control actions. Tuning power-performance and thermal models online. What you will deliver: Control software that turns intents into commands, with measured latency. Practical control systems using real-world data from experiments. A telemetry system that reports headroom and runs without breaking service-level limits in test scenarios. Work duties You will primarily devote yourself to your doctoral programme, which includes participation in research projects as well as third cycle courses, seminars and conferences. The PhD position consists of three main aspects, and the work duties include: research project, including authoring papers and traveling to conferences, seminars etc. postgraduate courses, given locally, nationally and internationally (90 ECTS credits) teaching (no more than 20%), including lab supervision and exam responsibilities. This part may also include departmental services, such as working with equality and diversity, arranging events etc Qualifications To be eligible for admission and employment as a doctoral student, you must fulfil the requirements below. Admission requirements A person meets the general admission requirements for third-cycle courses and study programmes if the applicant: has been awarded a second-cycle qualification, or has satisfied the requirements for courses comprising at least 240 credits of which at least 60 credits were awarded in the second cycle, or has acquired substantial equivalent knowledge in some other way in Sweden or abroad. A person meets the specific admission requirements for third cycle studies in Automatic Control if the applicant has: knowledge corresponding to course FRTF05 (previously FRT010) Automatic Control, Basic Course and a second cycle specialisation of relevance to the subject, and an MSc in engineering or corresponding qualification The standard MSCA eligibility criteria also apply: No doctoral degree. At the date of recruitment you must not yet hold a PhD. Mobility rule. You must not have lived or done your main activity (work or studies) in Sweden for more than 12 months in the 36 months just before you are recruited. Short stays such as holidays do not count. Additional requirements In order to complete the doctoral programme in question, the following are also required: Very good oral and written proficiency in English. Good language-analytical ability. Curiosity about research and teaching in general, and motivation for the particular research project. Creativity with good ability for cooperation and networking. Good ability to work independently and to formulate and tackle research problems. Good written and oral communication skills. Strong mathematical knowledge as demonstrated through university transcripts. Other qualifications (advantages) For the doctoral programme in question, the following are considered as other qualifications: Strong foundations in Machine learning and reinformement learning. Cloud computing and cloud technology. Low-level programming and Linux kernel experience. are meriting. Consideration will be given to good collaborative skills, drive and independence, and how the applicant,through experience and skills, is deemed to have the abilities necessary for successfully completing the third cycle programme. We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. More about working at Lund University on lu.se. https://www.lunduniversity.lu.se/about-university/work-lund-university About the employment The employment is a fixed-term employment at full time, starting as soon as possible or March 1, 2027, at the latest. Third cycle studies at LTH consist of full-time studies for 4 years. In the case of teaching and other departmental duties, the employment is extended accordingly. Doctoral studentships are regulated in the Higher Education Ordinance (1993:100), chapter 5, 1-7 §§. More about terms of employment for doctoral students on Lund University’s Staffpages. https://www.staff.lu.se/research-and-education/research-support/doctoral-education/terms-employment-doctoral-students Other We intend to offer employment to 1 PhD student. A doctoral student will receive a competitive monthly salary of 35 900 SEK with yearly raise. Those applicants who will be called for interview, might be asked to perform a small test to demonstrate basic competence. How to apply Applications shall be written in English and include: CV and a cover letter stating the reasons why you are interested in the doctoral programme/employment and in what way the research project corresponds to your interests and educational background. Copies of issued study certificates and/or awarded degree certificates. These must confirm that you meet the general and specific admission requirements for the doctoral programme and show that you have the subject knowledge required for the doctoral programme project. Other documents you wish to be considered (grade transcripts, contact information for your references, letters of recommendation, etc.) We welcome your application. LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2448 Contact Johan Eker, +46462228780, johan.eker@control.lth.se Union representative SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 08.Sep.2026 Last application date 23.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:964482/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Polymer Technology with a focus on Polymer Chemistry",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Polymer Technology with a focus on Polymer Chemistry Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Assistant Professor in Polymer Technology with a focus on Polymer Chemistry Login and apply Lund University, LTH, Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The Department of Chemistry at Lund University, Faculty of Engineering, invites applications for a position as Assistant Professor in Polymer technology with focus on polymer chemistry. Placed at the Centre for Analysis and Synthesis (CAS) at the Department of Chemistry, the recruited Assistant Professor will be supported by funding of a PhD student. The Department of Chemistry conducts research across all core areas of chemistry with a broad theme of research in support of a sustainable society. Focus areas include the environment, renewable resources, energy and health. Of special relevance are molecular design and synthesis, and understanding of the interplay between structure and properties, chemical and physical processes, and application performance. Advanced characterization of material properties by, e.g., electron microscopy and X-ray characterization (in particular at the synchrotron MAX IV) is also a focal area of the Department. Current research within polymer technology at CAS is mainly directed towards polymers for energy and the environment, including polymeric materials for energy storage and conversion, and sustainable biobased polymers. We synthesize and characterize new monomers, polymer structures, and materials to establish fundamental structure-property relationships relevant for specific application areas. In our work we emphasize a creative environment characterized by scientific excellence, diversity, generosity and freedom. We are now expanding and need to further strengthen our capacity and competence in polymer chemistry. In this context, the successful candidate may develop new synthetic pathways to meet demanding polymer properties for specific application areas. The choice of research approach, application area, etc., will be determined by the research interests of the candidate. Combinations of experimental techniques and theory are most welcome. The position therefore offers an excellent opportunity to develop an independent research programme at the interface of polymer chemistry, material science and sustainability, supported by a strong research environment and dedicated PhD-student funding. Subject Polymer Technology with a focus on Polymer Chemistry . Subject description Deep knowledge in polymer technology and polymer chemistry is crucial for the development, production, application and recyclability of the next generation of efficient and environmentally benign polymeric materials. Polymer technology is an interdisciplinary subject including the chemistry and physics of polymers, polymers from renewable resources, processing and use of polymer materials, and degradation and environmental aspects of polymer materials. Polymer chemistry comprises polymerisations and chemical modifications of polymers, combined with polymer characterisation to determine fundamental structure-property relationships. Work duties Employment as an assistant professor is a tenure track position, which aims for the holder to develop their independence as a researcher and educator. The work duties mainly involve research and teaching. The position includes the opportunity to undertake five weeks of training in teaching and learning in higher education. Work duties include: Research within the subject area. Teaching in the first, second and third cycles of studies. Supervision of degree projects and doctoral students. Actively seeking external research funding. Collaboration with industry and wider society. Administration related to the work duties listed above. Qualification requirements Qualification requirements for employment as an assistant professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an assistant professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. Assessment criteria For appointment to assistant professor, the following shall form the assessment criteria: A good ability to develop and carry out high-quality research. Teaching ability. Additional requirements Very good oral and written proficiency in English. Extensive documented experience within polymer chemistry, including the synthesis and characterization of structure and fundamental properties of polymers. Experience of postdoctoral research in a research environment other than the university at which the applicant obtained their PhD degree A submitted research plan (1500-2000 words) that can form the basis for external funding Collaborative skills, initiative and the ability to solve work tasks independently, as well as a demonstrated interest in leadership. Other qualifications Documented ability to acquire competitive external research funding. As part of the application, the applicant is to submit a research plan (1500-2000 words) clearly describing the research that will be conducted during the employment within the topic of Polymer Chemistry . The plan should also describe how the applicant’s research could complement and reinforce research at the Department of Chemistry. The quality of the research plan shall be given substantial weight in the selection of candidates. Terms of employment This is a full-time, fixed-term employment of 6 years. The employment is regulated in accordance with Chapter 4 Section 12a§ HEA. The purpose of the appointment is for the teacher to be given the opportunity to develop research autonomy and acquire the scholarly and teaching qualifications required for eligibility for appointment as a senior lecturer. We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. Read more on the University website about being a Lund University employee: https://www.lunduniversity.lu.se/about-university/work-lund-university. Instructions on how to apply Applications shall be written in English. Please draw up the application in accordance with LTH’s Academic qualifications portfolio – see link below. Upload the application as PDF-files in the recruitment system. Read more: http://www.lth.se/english/working-at-lth/to-apply-for-academic-positions-at-lth/ We welcome your application! ***************************************************************** Promotion to Senior Lecturer in Polymer Technology with a focus on Polymer Chemistry During the period of employment, an assistant professor can apply for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer if he or she has the required qualifications listed below, and is deemed suitable. An assistant professor can only apply for promotion once. Qualification requirements Appointment to senior lecturer requires that the applicant has: A PhD or corresponding research competence or professional expertise considered important with regard to the subject matter of the post and the work duties it will involve. Demonstrated teaching expertise. Completed five weeks of training in higher education teaching and learning, or acquired equivalent knowledge by other means. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria specify the aspects to be considered, and the level to be achieved, for the assessment criteria to be deemed fulfilled. The following assessment criteria must be fulfilled for appointment to senior lecturer: A good national and international standing as a researcher. The requirement for international experience shall be assessed with consideration to the character and traditions of the subject. Good teaching ability, including a good ability to conduct, develop and lead teaching and other educational activities on different levels and using a variety of teaching methods. An ability to supervise doctoral students to achieve a PhD. An ability to collaborate with wider society and communicate his or her activities. A general ability to lead and develop activities. Additional requirements Demonstrated scientific independence, shown among other things by several publications published without their previous supervisors or mentors, where the applicant has clearly had a leading (PI) role. Good ability to collaborate, independence and drive. Sufficient knowledge of Swedish to carry out administrative and educational tasks that require an understanding of Swedish. Progression in research and teaching expertise. Ability to actively, independently, and successfully seek external funding as main applicant. This means that the applicant has received multi-year research grants from large national or international funders such as ERC, Wallenberg Foundations, Swedish Research Council, Formas, Swedish Energy Agency, etc. If the applicant has not received such funding as main applicant, they must be able to show very good evaluations from the application process. LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Permanent position Contract type Full time First day of employment As soon as possible Salary Monthly Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 % City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2526 Contact Ola Wendt, +46462228153, ola.wendt@chem.lu.se Union representative SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2229364, kansli@saco-s.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 09.Sep.2026 Last application date 03.Nov.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:930481/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in technical GIS",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer in technical GIS Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Senior Lecturer in technical GIS Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Research and education at the Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences (MGeo) addresses fundamental and applied questions on the Earth’s past, present and future climate and environment, including people’s interactions with the natural world and consequences for human wellbeing. MGeo advances and deploys cutting-edge methods, models and technologies in environmental science, quaternary sciences, bedrock geology, paleontology, physical geography, biodiversity and ecosystem science, remote sensing, Geographic Information Science (GIS), and computational science for health and environment, to study processes spanning from the microscopic to the planetary, across all time scales. Lund University welcomes applicants with different backgrounds and experiences. Gender equality, equal opportunities and diversity are fundamental principles in all parts of the University’s activities. Find more reasons why Lund University and the Faculty of Science are right for you here and here , and learn more about what it is like to Working in Lund , Moving to Lund and Living in Lund . Research and education at the Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences (MGeo) address fundamental and applied questions on the Earth’s past, present and future climate and environment, including people’s interactions with the natural world and consequences for human wellbeing. MGeo advances and deploys cutting-edge methods, models and technologies in environmental science, quaternary sciences, bedrock geology, paleontology, physical geography, biodiversity and ecosystem science, remote sensing, Geographic Information Science (GIS), and computational science for health and environment, to study processes spanning from the microscopic to the planetary, across all time scales. Job description The successful candidate is expected to contribute to the department’s core activities in research, teaching and collaboration, as well as, where appropriate, academic leadership roles. The post involves research in geographic information science, with an emphasis on the technical aspects of GIS. The successful candidate is expected to actively seek and secure external research funding, publish in peer-reviewed scientific journals, and contribute to the development of the department’s research environment, including interdisciplinary collaborations. The duties also include teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels in geographic information science (GIS), with a particular focus on technically oriented courses such as geographic databases, Web GIS, GIS algorithms and data structures, geospatial artificial intelligence (GeoAI), and the collection and analysis of 3D geodata. The position includes course coordination, course development and participation in pedagogical development within the subject area. Within the framework of outreach, the successful candidate is expected to engage with the wider community, including public authorities, industry and other societal stakeholders, and to contribute to the application of GIS and geospatial technologies in relevant societal areas. The duties may also include participation in departmental and faculty-wide administrative and management-related tasks, such as committee work, programme development and coordination assignments. The allocation of time between research, teaching, outreach and administrative duties follows the faculty’s working hours model, under which teaching normally accounts for approximately 25–35% of a full-time position, with the remaining time divided between research, outreach and other duties. The content, distribution and scope of the duties may change over time in line with the development of the organisation, strategic priorities and external conditions. To support the establishment of a research group, resources equivalent to a PhD student position will be attached to the post. Qualification requirements A person is qualified to be employed as a senior lecturer if they have demonstrated teaching expertise and been awarded a PhD in subject matter of relevance to the position, or equivalent research expertise, in accordance with the Higher Education Ordinance, Chapter 4 Section 4. For this position, applicants must hold a PhD in Geographic Information Systems, Geographic Information Science, Geoinformatics, Engineering Surveying, Computer Science, Information Systems, or a related or equivalent subject area. The position requires at least five weeks’ training in teaching and learning in higher education or corresponding knowledge that has been obtained in some other way. An exception to this requirement may be made in special cases. Applicants who do not possess training in teaching and learning in higher education are to undergo such training within two years of taking up the position. Teaching staff at Lund University are required to be suitable for the position and to have the general ability to fulfil their work duties well. Assessment criteria The requirements for the position are: The applicant must demonstrate a strong academic background in the technical aspects of geographic information science (GIS). Academic competence must be evidenced by publications in reputable, peer-reviewed scientific journals. The applicant must demonstrate a proven ability to successfully secure external research funding in a competitive environment, as well as experience of leading their own research projects. The applicant must demonstrate documented experience of teaching at advanced level in GIS, particularly in areas such as geographic databases, web GIS, GIS algorithms and data structures, geospatial artificial intelligence (GeoAI), and the collection and analysis of 3D geodata. The applicant must have experience of teaching at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels. The applicant must demonstrate experience of supervising undergraduates and PhD students. The applicant must demonstrate a strong ability to engage with the wider community, including experience of applying GIS and geospatial technologies in areas such as the environment and climate, urban planning, health and epidemiology, and disaster management, preferably in collaboration with public sector bodies, industry or other societal stakeholders. The applicant must have a very good command of spoken and written English. The applicant must have experience of research and/or teaching outside Lund University. The applicant must have very good collaborative skills and well-developed communication skills. Merits for the position are: Experience and knowledge of geospatial artificial intelligence (GeoAI) and/or digital twins. Leadership skills, including experience of leading research projects or research teams. Proven experience of interdisciplinary collaboration. Experience of developing innovative teaching methods in GIS. Active participation in international research and education networks. Good command of Swedish, both spoken and written. Experience of distance learning at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level. Personal qualities The applicant should demonstrate creativity, strong drive and the ability to familiarise themselves with new fields of research. The ability to share knowledge and contribute to a collegial and collaborative academic environment is of great importance. The applicant must have the ability to interact effectively with research, teaching and administrative staff, both internally and externally, including stakeholders in academia, industry and the public sector. Further considerations As much attention will be given to the assessment of teaching expertise as to the assessment of research expertise. When appointing teaching staff, Lund University’s Appointment Rules apply, in addition to the provisions set out in the Higher Education Ordinance. For a non-Swedish speaking staff members, language level B2* must be achieved in Swedish within three years. *Common European Framework of Reference for Language, CEFR. What we offer Lund University is a public authority which means that you receive special benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension. Read more on the University website about working with us. Design and content of the application We recommend you write your application in English, as it may be reviewed by international external experts. The application is to be completed according to the Faculty of Science’s application instructions for teaching positions . Upload the application to the recruitment system as PDF files. Please note that it is very important that you follow the application instructions. An application that is incomplete will be rejected. Within the Faculty of Science research and education is conducted within Astronomy, Biology, Physics, Geosciences, Chemistry, Mathematics, medical radiation physics, physical geography and Environmental Sciences. The Faculty of Science is organized into eight departments, gathered in the northern campus area in Lund. The Faculty of Science has approximately 1900 students, 330 PhD students and 730 employees. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Permanent position Contract type Full time First day of employment As soon as possible Salary Monthly Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/1439 Contact Ali Mansourian Ali Mansourian, Professor, ali.mansourian@mgeo.lu.se Ying Pan, Recruitment Coordinator, +46462228917, ying.pan@science.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 08.Sep.2026 Last application date 04.Nov.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:943541/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Experimental Medical Science",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer in Experimental Medical Science Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Senior Lecturer in Experimental Medical Science Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Employment This is permanent employment as a senior lecturer in experimental medical science, with placement at either the Department of Experimental Medical Science (EMV) or the Department of Laboratory Medicine (ILM). The department to which the employment is attached will be decided upon appointment. Duties The main responsibilities will be to lead and develop undergraduate and second cycle education in experimental medicine. The successful candidate will therefore be expected to be prepared to take on key leadership roles at course or program level within the Medical Programme or Biomedicine Programmes. The position also involves teaching and evaluation within the Biomedicine Programmes and/or the first cycle of the Medical Programme (semesters 1-5) using various forms of active learning. As a guideline, teaching, educational management and educational development work is expected to account for approximately 70% of the time over time. Tasks related to third cycle studies may also be included. The teaching role is therefore the main focus of employment. In addition to teaching, the position involves active research in experimental medicine. The position also includes the responsibilities normally associated with the position of Senior Lecturer, such as administrative duties and internal and external elected offices at the national and international levels. Other duties may also be required. Eligibility A person is eligible for employment as a senior lecturer under Chapter 4, Section 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance if he/she has demonstrated teaching ability and has either a doctoral degree or equivalent academic or other professional competence relevant to the subject matter of the employment and the duties to be performed in the position. The applicant must have completed a course in higher education pedagogy lasting at least five weeks. Should the appointee be assessed to have valid reasons for not having completed a five-week course in higher education pedagogy upon taking up the position, they must complete such a course within two years of taking up the position (see Lund University’s Staff Regulations ). Language proficiency equivalent to at least level C2 in Swedish, as per the Council of Europe’s proficiency scale, is a requirement for employment. For teaching staff members at Lund University, the general requirements are that they are suitable for the role and possess the other abilities necessary to carry out their duties effectively. l Assessment criteria The applicant must have demonstrated teaching, academic, and administrative competence relevant to the discipline; particular emphasis will be placed on teaching competence during the review process. The applicant must demonstrate teaching expertise relevant to the subject area, both in teaching and in supervision at undergraduate, postgraduate and doctoral levels. This is demonstrated by a proven ability to deliver teaching and summative assessment through various forms of active learning, in accordance with the Faculty of Medicine’s educational plan. The ability to lead and develop university-level education at first- and second cycle level is advantageous. The candidate’s own development work in higher education pedagogy or research projects is also advantageous. In addition, the applicant must demonstrate research expertise by conducting his/her own current research. Research expertise is demonstrated by publications in international scientific journals, competitive grants, and experience in national and international research collaborations. In addition, research expertise is demonstrated by relevant research programs and the potential for long-term career development. The candidate must demonstrate strong leadership skills and the ability to work collaboratively and externally with the broader community. When employing teaching staff, in addition to the consequences of the Higher Education Ordinance , Lund University’s Staff Regulations and the Faculty of Medicine’s general requirements and assessment criteria for Senior Lecturer shall apply. Further information For further information, please contact Dean Maria Björkqvist, tel. +46 72 700 71 63, maria.bjorkqvist@med.lu.se or Head of Department Karolin Hansén Nord, tel. +46 70 283 1827, karolin.hansen_nord@med.lu.se or Head of Department Tomas Deierborg, tel. +46 70 970 8212, tomas.deierborg@med.lu.se Further information on the application process can be obtained from the recruitment coordinator, Eva A Nilsson, tel. +46 46 222 17 89, eva_a.nilsson@med.lu.se The employment will be filled following a recommendation from the Medical Academic Appointment Board. Application procedure Applications for this employment must be submitted online via Lund University’s application portal by 27 November 2026 at the latest. Applications must be submitted in accordance with the Faculty of Medicine's guidelines for applicants for faculty positions (see \"Summary of Qualifications and CV\"). The university applies a system of individual salary-setting. Please state your salary expectations in your application. Lund University welcomes applicants from a variety of backgrounds and with a range of experiences. We regard gender equality and diversity as a strength and an asset. We look forward to receiving your application! The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Permanent position Contract type Full time First day of employment Enligt överenskommelse Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/1950 Contact Maria Björkqvist, Dekan, maria.bjorkqvist@med.lu.se Karolin Hansén Nord, Prefekt ILM, karolin.hansen_nord@med.lu.se Tomas Deierborg, Prefekt EMV, tomas.deierborg@med.lu.se Eva Nilsson, Rekryteringskoordinator, eva_a.nilsson@med.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 03.Sep.2026 Last application date 27.Nov.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:967814/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Researcher in Photocatalysis",
      "description": "Researcher in Photocatalysis Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Researcher in Photocatalysis Login and apply Lunds universitet Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Subject description The research involves developing photocatalytic reactions for the reduction of CO2 to fuels and the reduction of protons to hydrogen gas. Work duties The work involves optimizing the respective photocatalytic reactions that have been develop in the group using iron N-heterocyclic carbene complex as the photosensitizer together with different CO2-reduction catalysts. The work is mainly laborative, but writing of texts aimed for scientific publications is also a part of the work duties. Qualification requirements Applicants must have: A PhD or equivalent research qualification within organic/inorganic chemistry. Post-doc research qualifications within the area of photocatalysis involving transition metal complexes. Very good oral and written proficiency in English. Assessment criteria and other qualifications Previous research  experience using iron N-heterocycklic complexes in the context of CO2 rduction and H2 evolution is meritorious. Consideration will also be given to good collaborative skills, drive and independence, and how the applicant’s experience and skills complement and strengthen ongoing research within the department, and how they stand to contribute to its future development. Terms of employment Fixed-term employment, full-time employment, length of employment: From November 18 2026 to February 17 2027. Instructions on how to apply Applications shall be written in English and be compiled into a PDF-file containing: résumé/CV, including a list of publications, copy of the doctoral degree certificate, and other certificates/grades that you wish to be considered. Within the Faculty of Science research and education is conducted within Astronomy, Biology, Physics, Geosciences, Chemistry, Mathematics, medical radiation physics, physical geography and Environmental Sciences. The Faculty of Science is organized into eight departments, gathered in the northern campus area in Lund. The Faculty of Science has approximately 1900 students, 330 PhD students and 730 employees. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment 2026-11-18 Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2641 Contact Kenneth Wärnmark, +46462228217, kenneth.warnmark@chem.lu.se Erik Andersson Jardby, +46462228128, erik.andersson_jardby@chem.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 15.Sep.2026 Last application date 29.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:967149/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Required qualifications for the position are:",
      "description": "Research Engineer in AI for Media and Language Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Research Engineer in AI for Media and Language Login and apply Lunds universitet, LTH, Institutionen för datavetenskap Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position is at the Department of Computer Science within the division Robotics and Semantic Systems (RSS). The RSS division focuses on research and teaching in AI, Machine Learning, Robotics and Robotic Learning, Human-Robot Interaction, and Natural Language Processing. Together with the Dept. of Automatic Control, the division operates RobotLab LTH, which gives access to many types of robots. You will collaborate closely with ML experts from both academia and industry, support international benchmarking challenges such as GENEA and VOT, and contribute to the annual WASP summer school on multimodal modelling of human communication. The position also gives access to first-class computing infrastructure, including the WARA Common computing centre and the Kebnekaise and Berzelius supercomputers, and offers opportunities to co-author scientific publications and shape the development of AI in media and language. Your workplace is on Lund University's cohesive campus, where everything is close by, making it easy to meet, collaborate and exchange knowledge. The department strives for an open, inclusive and supportive work environment where colleagues with different backgrounds and perspectives learn from each other and celebrate each other's successes. More information about the Department of Computer Science More information about RSS More information about the RobotLab LTH More information about WARA M&L We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. Read more on the University website about being a Lund University employee Work for us at Lund University Work duties As a Research Engineer in WARA Media and Language (WARA M&L), your primary duties are to design, implement and host a Machine Learning platform for the research community within WARA M&L. The platform will be supported by the resources provided from WARA Common, a sister arena within WASP and HPC2N (high performance computing cluster). The work covers all aspects of the platform construction: Interviews with partners from academia and industry, elaboration of needs, design and implementation of the system, management of data sets, hosting and sharing of data, providing data annotation services, benchmarking machine learning challenges and user support. The work also involves providing technical support for the annual GENEA challenge and Online VOT benchmark. Apart from benchmarking challenges, you will also be working on the annual WASP Summer school event, where you will be implementing and developing student projects on multimodal modelling of human communication. Qualifications Required qualifications for the position are: Bachelor's degree or higher in Computer Science or a related field Practical and theoretical experience in Machine Learning Experience in software development Demonstrated ability to set up, configure, and maintain distributed development environments that support collaborative AI projects Experience designing and operating AI platform components, including resource management, data hosting, and data-sharing services Solid grounding in collecting, processing, and structuring data for AI workflows Experience with Linux-based system administration Programming experience, including shell scripting, Python, and working with APIs Basic knowledge of virtualization, networking, and computer security Experience working with containers (e.g., Docker, Singularity) and version control/CI/CD tools (e.g., GitHub) Good collaborative skills and the ability to work in environments with both academic and industrial partners Ability to work independently, in a structured manner and with a problem-solving approach Good communication skills and the ability to provide support to users with varying technical backgrounds Additional qualifications for the position are: Familiarity with front-end, back-end, JS frameworks, and database technologies Experience conducting scientific research in Machine Learning, AI, or a closely related field Experience providing technical support for ML/AI infrastructure, including troubleshooting for end-users (e.g., researchers) and authoring technical documentation Experience as a teaching assistant or in leading technical workshops and mentoring students on project-based work Experience with Unity, Blender, Unreal Engine, or Omniverse Experience with K8s, Docker Registry, Docker Swarm, or MLOps/AIOps frameworks Pedagogical ability, for example through experience of supervision, workshops or project-based student work Ability to contribute to an open, inclusive and supportive work environment Initiative and an interest in contributing to the development of research infrastructure and future AI applications Consideration will also be given to how the applicant’s experience and skills complement and strengthen ongoing research within the department, and how they stand to contribute to its future development. Further information The position is a fixed term position for one year at 100% of full time, starting on October 1st 2026 or by agreement. How to apply A complete application must contain: A personal motivation letter describing why you are suitable for the advertised position Curriculum Vitae (CV) Other relevant information, such as documentation of software development experience, or publication list. Contact information to two persons willing to give references The application must be submitted via the Varbi e-recruitment system no later than 2026-09-29. Further information can be obtained from Sonny Vu, e-mail xuan-son.vu@cs.lth.se. Lund University welcomes applicants with diverse backgrounds and experiences. We regard gender equality and diversity as a strength and an asset. We look forward to your application! LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Special fixed-term employment Contract type Full time Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2617 Published 15.Sep.2026 Last application date 29.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:968821/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Associate researcher in Leukaemia Cell Quiescence and Treatment Resistance",
      "description": "Associate researcher in Leukaemia Cell Quiescence and Treatment Resistance Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Associate researcher in Leukaemia Cell Quiescence and Treatment Resistance Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The research group is part of the Division of Molecular Medicine and Gene Therapy within the Faculty of Medicine, Lund University. In childhood B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (B-ALL), a subset of leukaemic cells survives chemotherapy by entering a dormant, quiescent state. These cells are thought to contribute to minimal residual disease and ultimately drive relapse. The group investigates the mechanisms underpinning this process using a human induced pluripotent stem cell (iPSC)-derived bone marrow organoid model engrafted with primary ALL cells, enabling the study of leukaemia-niche interactions that govern entry into quiescence. This platform is combined with an expressed barcode system that enables simultaneous tracking of lineage, cell state, and cell-cycle dynamics at single-cell resolution. The project is conducted in collaboration with the UCL Cancer Institute, where Prof. Tariq Enver holds his primary appointment. The research team is co-led by Prof. Enver, who is also Visiting Professor at Lund University, and Dr. Virginia Turati, and includes doctoral students and postdoctoral researchers. We are committed to maintaining a positive, inclusive and respectful working environment, where colleagues are treated with consideration and professionalism. What we offer Lund University is a public authority, which means that you will have special benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension. We also have a flexitime agreement that creates good conditions for work/life balance. Read more on the University's website about being a Lund University employee, Work at Lund University. Work duties and areas of responsibility The position focuses on investigating how and why leukaemic cells evade chemotherapy by entering a quiescent state, and on developing the experimental tools required to track their fate. Duties include: Implementing, optimising and maintaining the group's human bone marrow organoid model, including engraftment of primary ALL cells and chemotherapy-exposure experiments Isolating and characterising quiescent, chemotherapy-resistant (label-retaining) ALL cells using flow cytometry, cell sorting and functional assays Applying and further developing the group's expressed lineage-barcoding platform, including lentiviral transduction of ALL cells for simultaneous lineage, cell-state and cell-cycle tracking Performing molecular profiling of dormant, therapy-resistant leukaemic cell states Maintaining rigorous experimental records and contributing to the group's standards for quality, reproducibility and data management Participating in manuscript preparation, scientific communication and presentation of results at national and international conferences Co-supervising master's and doctoral students within the research group Qualifications Requirements for the position A doctoral degree in cell biology, molecular biology, stem cell biology, haematology, cancer biology, or a closely related field Demonstrated hands-on experience with human stem/progenitor cell culture, such as iPSC-derived systems, primary haematopoietic cells or primary leukaemia samples Experience with flow cytometry and cellular functional assays Ability to independently plan, execute and analyse research projects Excellent written and spoken English Additional qualifications Experience with 3D culture, organoid models, or tissue-engineered systems Experience with lentiviral or other viral vector-based genetic engineering of mammalian cells Experience with CRISPR/Cas9-mediated genome editing Experience in leukaemia biology, haematopoiesis, stem cell biology, or cancer therapy-resistance research Experience supervising, mentoring, or training students and junior researchers Previous experience of research in similar projects Further information The employment is a fixed-term position for 12 months on full-time under the Special Fixed-Term Employment Act (SÄVA). The starting date is 15 November, or according to agreement. How to apply You apply for the position via the University's recruitment system. The application is to include a personal letter describing how you meet the qualification requirements and why you are interested in the position. The application is also to include a CV with a list of publications, a general description of previous research work, a copy of your doctoral degree and any other documents to which you wish to draw attention (copies of degree certificates, grade transcripts, details of referees, letters of recommendation, etc.). The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. The Department of Laboratory Medicine is one of six departments at Lund University’s Faculty of Medicine. We conduct leading research, both broad and specialised, within the field of laboratory medicine, focusing on the onset of disease and how we can improve diagnostics and treatment for patients. Some of our strongest areas with world-class research deal with cancer, genetics and blood. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment 2026-11-15 or according to agreement Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2666 Contact Jonas Larsson, Recruiting manager, +46462220580, jonas.larsson@med.lu.se Virginia Turati, Project leader, virginia.turati@med.lu.se Adam Niekrasz, HR-partner, +46462220000, adam.niekrasz@med.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 15.Sep.2026 Last application date 30.Sep.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:968052/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Project Assistent in the department of Translational Oncogenomics",
      "description": "Project Assistent in the department of Translational Oncogenomics Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Project Assistent in the department of Translational Oncogenomics Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The Translational Oncogenomics research group is seeking a Master’s-level Project Assistant with experience in breast cancer research and bioinformatics skills to contribute to research projects within the field. The position is based at the Department of Clinical Sciences Lund, Division of Oncology, located at Medicon Village. The working environment is international and interdisciplinary, with a focus on cancer biology, genetics, genomics, oncology, and bioinformatics. The group consists of researchers, PhD students, and bioinformaticians, and you will work closely with experienced colleagues in a dynamic research setting. Within our research group, we are committed to maintaining a positive work environment characterized by mutual respect and consideration. We continuously strive to create conditions that promote job satisfaction, professional development, and participation for all employees. Within the research group, we value a positive work environment characterized by respect and consideration in our relationships. We continuously strive to create conditions that promote job satisfaction, development, and inclusion for all employees. We are now recruiting a full-time Project Assistant (100%) for the period 2026-10-01 to 2027-03-31. We offer Lund University is a government agency, which means you will receive specific benefits, generous annual leave, and an advantageous occupational pension. We also have a flexible working hours agreement that supports a healthy work–life balance. Read more on the university’s website about working at Lund University: Work with us. Duties and responsibilities The position is full-time and work is primarily carried out on-site. Supervision meetings will be held every one to two weeks to provide guidance and support. Responsibilities include conducting cancer research and bioinformatics analyses, handling biological samples, analysing data, compiling research results, reviewing scientific literature, preparing figures, and contributing to scientific writing. Other duties may be assigned within the scope of the role. Other duties may be added within the scope of the role. Qualifications Requirements for the position are: Master’s degree in Molecular Biology or a related field Excellent proficiency in English, both spoken and written Experience in breast cancer research and blood plasma processing Experience in bioinformatics analysis of clinical and genomic data Experience working with RStudio and software packages such as ggplot2, survival, and GenVisR Meritorious: Knowledge of scientific writing and reference management using EndNote Experience in figure generation using BioRender Knowledge of code and results organization using Quarto Other The employment is a fixed-term special appointment (SÄVA) at 100% for the period of 2026.10.01 - 2027.03.31. Desired start date is October 1, 2026, or as agreed. How to apply The position is applied for through the university’s recruitment system. The application should include a personal letter explaining why you are interested in the position and how your qualifications match the requirements. The application should also include a CV, degree certificate or equivalent, and any other documents you wish to submit (copies of transcripts, contact information for references, letters of recommendation, etc.). Welcome with your application! The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Special fixed-term employment Employment expires 2027-03-31 Contract type Full time First day of employment 2026-10-01 Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 % City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2650 Contact Lina Bull, HR-partner, lina.bull@med.lu.se Lao Saal, Huvudhandledare, lao.saal@med.lu.se Union representative OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, kansli@saco-s.lu.se, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 17.Sep.2026 Last application date 01.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:970019/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral position in food technology with specialisation in dairy and membrane processes",
      "description": "Postdoctoral position in food technology with specialisation in dairy and membrane processes Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Postdoctoral position in food technology with specialisation in dairy and membrane processes Login and apply This content is hosted by a third party. By showing the external content you accept the cookie terms and conditions of www.youtube.com Lunds universitet Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. The Department of Process and Life Science Engineering (PLE) at Lund University is located at Kemicentrum, one of the Nordics' largest centers for education and research in the field of chemistry. PLE has around 150 employees, of which around 65 are PhD students. The department offers a broad competence in areas such as chemical engineering, food technology, biotechnology and applied microbiology. The research and education at PLE spans the entire subject of chemistry and includes both undergraduate and graduate education. The Department of Process Engineering and Applied Biosciences (PLE) at Lund University works actively to ensure a good working environment through several initiatives and routines. PLE encourages open communication and collaboration between employees, students and management to continuously improve and have regular meetings and dialogues to discuss workplace safety. Description of the workplace You will be part of the dairy group at the Division of Food and Pharma, in close collaboration with the membrane group at the Division of Chemical Engineering. The dairy group conducts research and education in dairy technology and dairy processes, with a focus on milk quality and how raw material and process conditions affect the properties of dairy products. As a postdoctoral researcher, you will become part of an interdisciplinary research environment with expertise in food technology, dairy technology, process technology and membrane technology, where the membrane group contributes expertise in the development and optimisation of membrane processes for industrial applications. Subject description The position is in food technology with a focus on the development and evaluation of processes for the circular utilisation of by-products from dairy production. The research includes membrane-based separation and concentration processes, including ultrafiltration, nanofiltration and electrodialysis, as well as processing of protein-rich fractions through dairy processes. Particular emphasis is placed on the relationship between process conditions, composition, structure and functionality of milk and whey proteins, and how these affect the quality, texture, stability and nutritional value of fermented dairy products. The work includes process development and characterisation of raw materials and intermediate products, as well as evaluation of functional properties and product quality in food systems. Work duties The main duties involved in a post-doctoral position is to conduct research. Teaching may also be included, but up to no more than 20% of working hours. The position includes the opportunity for three weeks of training in higher education teaching and learning. The purpose of the position is to develop the independence as a researcher and to create the opportunity of further development. You will have particular responsibility for experimental work at laboratory and pilot scale in the development and evaluation of dairy and membrane processes. The work will be carried out in close collaboration between academia and industry and includes practical process work, sample handling, analysis and interpretation of results in relation to the project objectives. Detailed description of the work duties, such as: plan, conduct and document laboratory- and pilot-scale experiments in dairy and membrane processes, develop and evaluate membrane-based processes and processing strategies for recovery and reuse of components from dairy by-products, prepare, process and analyse dairy-based samples and evaluate relevant compositional, physicochemical and functional properties, process, analyse and interpret experimental data using appropriate statistical methods, contribute to scientific publications, project reports, presentations and other communication of research results, participate in project meetings and collaborate with academic and industrial partners, collect and transport samples from dairy production facilities when needed for the project experiments, contribute to supervision, teaching, applications for external research funding, research development and related administration. Qualification requirements Appointment to a post-doctoral position requires that the applicant has a PhD, or an international degree deemed equivalent to a PhD, within the subject of the position. The certificate proving the qualification requirement is met, must be received before the employment decision is made. Priority will be given to candidates who have graduated no more than three years ago before the last day for application. Under special circumstances, the doctoral degree can have been completed earlier. Additional requirements: You have a very good oral and written proficiency in English. You have knowledge of membrane-based separation and concentration processes, e.g. ultrafiltration, nanofiltration, or electrodialysis, as well as their application in the processing industry, such as food-, biotechnology, and pharmaceutical industries. You have practical experience in experimental work involving membrane processes. You have experience in independently planning, conducting, and documenting laboratory work within for example food, biotechnology, or process engineering applications. You have experience in analysing experimental data and presenting results in scientific contexts. You possess strong analytical skills and a structured approach to work. You have good collaborative skills and the ability to work in interdisciplinary research environments as well as with external collaborators. Assessment criteria This is a career development position primarily focused on research. The position is intended as an initial step in a career, and the assessment of the applicants will primarily be based on their research qualifications and potential as researchers. Particular emphasis will be placed on research skills within the subject. For appointments to a post-doctoral position, the following shall form the assessment criteria: A good ability to develop and conduct high-quality research. Teaching skills. Other qualifications: It is considered an advantage if you have knowledge and experience in dairy technology, dairy processing, or the structure, functionality, and quality of fermented dairy products, knowledge of protein structure, aggregation, and functionality in food systems, particularly milk proteins, experience with relevant analytical methods for the characterization of food, protein, or colloidal systems, knowledge and experience in statistical experimental design, modelling, programming, or multivariate data analysis, experience in process development and pilot-scale trials involving membrane and/or food processing technologies, experience of collaboration with industrial partners, a Category B driving license. Consideration will also be given to how the applicant’s experience and skills complement and strengthen ongoing research within the department, and how they stand to contribute to its future development. We offer Ready to shape the future of research? Learn more about why Lund University might be the right choice for you here , and learn more about working in Sweden here . These films will also give you more information about Working in Lund , Moving to Lund and Living in Lund . Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. Further information This is a full-time, fixed-term employment of two years. The period of employment is determined in accordance with the agreement “Avtal om tidsbegränsad anställning som postdoktor” (“Agreement on fixed-term employment as a post-doctoral fellow”). How to apply Applications are to be written in English and submitted via the University’s recruitment system. The application should include a CV with a list of publications, and a personal letter justifying your interest in the position and how it matches your qualificationst. The application should also include a degree certificate or equivalent and any other document to which you would like to draw attention (copies of grade transcripts, details of referees, letters of recommendation, etc.) We welcome your application! LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment 2026-11-01 or as agreed Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2706 Contact Frida Lewerentz, Associate senior lecturer, +46(0)724615476, frida.lewerentz@ple.lth.se Mia Hedin, mia.hedin@ple.lth.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 17.Sep.2026 Last application date 04.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:968464/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Computer Science with focus on AI engineering",
      "description": "Doctoral student in Computer Science with focus on AI engineering Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Doctoral student in Computer Science with focus on AI engineering Login and apply Lund University, LTH, Department of Computer Science Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The doctoral student will be employed at the Department of Computer Science, where research on engineering of large-scale software-intensive systems is conducted in the Software Engineering unit. The unit is a world leader on empirical software engineering. In addition to conventional software, the scope includes engineering of AI enabled systems (primarily ML and LLM), and thus MLOps (Machine Learning Operations), datacentric AI, and legal and ethical aspects of AI. The empirical research catalyzes industry-academia collaboration and cross-disciplinary initiatives, in which the doctoral student may conduct their studies. Particularly, this position is funded within the ELLIIT strategic research area (Excellence Center at Linköping – Lund in Information Technology) where a twin doctoral student will address the same topic from a business perspective. The student will also be affiliated with the NextG2Com industrial competence center, in the research theme on Integrated Data Engineering and Machine Learning. More about ELLIIT and the project More about NextG2Com Being a doctoral student As a doctoral student, you are both admitted as a student and employed at Lund University. As a doctoral student, you will be trained in a scientific approach. In short, you will be trained to think critically and analytically, to solve problems independently using the right methods, and to develop an awareness of research ethics. In addition, you will have the opportunity to work on projects, to develop your leadership and pedagogical skills. Throughout your studies, you will be guided by supervisors. Doctoral studies end with a thesis and a doctoral degree. More about being a doctoral student at LTH on lth.se . Subject and project description The study subject is computer science with a focus on AI engineering, i.e. engineering of systems containing AI-based features (LLM or ML). Application domains range from agentic software engineering to industrial control systems. Specifically, it addresses the issue of using general vs. specialized models (e.g. per task, company, country or domain). From a technical perspective, there are a multitude of factors to trade across the range of generalization–specialization, including model performance, energy consumption, training data supply, and integrity of data. The project aims to address AI engineering in business-to-business (B2B) contexts in various industry domains, as well as open source AI models. We perform empirical studies of industry practice for in-depth problem understanding. The project aims to design solutions for technical specialization and ecosystem governance. In particular, trade-offs between model size, data needs, computing costs, privacy and secrecy will be addressed. Work duties You will primarily devote yourself to your doctoral programme, which includes participation in research projects as well as third cycle courses, seminars and conferences. Since the project is conducted in the context of the ELLIIT and NextG2Com initiatives, you will interact with corresponding academic and industrial partners. The work duties include: Research within the project topic Collaboration with partners in ELLIIT and NextG2Com Participation in international research conferences Taking third cycle courses Participation in teaching and other departmental work (however, a maximum of 20% of working hours). Qualifications To be eligible for admission and employment as a doctoral student, you must fulfil the requirements below. Admission requirements A person meets the general admission requirements for third-cycle courses and study programmes if the applicant: has been awarded a second-cycle qualification, or has satisfied the requirements for courses comprising at least 240 credits of which at least 60 credits were awarded in the second cycle, or has acquired substantially equivalent knowledge in some other way in Sweden or abroad. A person meets the specific admission requirements for third cycle studies in Computer Science if the applicant has: at least 60 credits from M.Sc. level courses that are relevant for this topic, or an MSc in Engineering in Computer Science and Engineering, Electrical Engineering, Information and Communication Technology, Engineering Physics or Engineering Mathematics. Additional requirements In order to complete the doctoral programme in question, the following are also required: very good knowledge of English, spoken and written good knowledge about software and AI engineering theory and practice good knowledge about machine learning ability to collaborate with industry partners good written and oral communication skills good ability to cooperate good ability to work independently and to formulate and tackle research problems. Other qualifications (advantages) For the doctoral programme in question, the following are considered as other qualifications: relevant industrial experience working experience as a software engineer contributions to open source software, data commons and open source AI models knowledge about qualitative research methods knowledge about design science research methods We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. More about working at Lund University on lu.se . About the employment The employment is a fixed-term employment at full time, starting as soon as possible, however no later than 2026-12-15. Third cycle studies at LTH consist of full-time studies for 4 years. In the case of teaching and other departmental duties, the employment is extended accordingly. Doctoral studentships are regulated in the Higher Education Ordinance (1993:100), chapter 5, 1-7 §§. More about terms of employment for doctoral students on Lund University’s Staffpages. How to apply Applications shall be written in English and include: CV and a personally written cover letter stating the reasons why you are interested in the doctoral programme/employment and in what way the research project corresponds to your interests and educational background. Copies of issued study certificates and/or awarded degree certificates. These must confirm that you meet the general and specific admission requirements for the doctoral programme and show that you have the subject knowledge required for the doctoral programme project. Other documents you wish to be considered (grade transcripts, contact information for your references, letters of recommendation, etc.) We welcome your application. LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position First day of employment As soon as possible by agreement, however no later than March 1, 2027 Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2660 Contact Per Runeson, per.runeson@cs.lth.se Union representative OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, kansli@saco-s.lu.se, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 17.Sep.2026 Last application date 08.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:968016/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Electrical Engineering focusing on Cybersecurity",
      "description": "Doctoral student in Electrical Engineering focusing on Cybersecurity Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Doctoral student in Electrical Engineering focusing on Cybersecurity Login and apply Lund University, LTH, Electrical and Information Technology Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The Division for Secure and Networked Systems within the Department of Electrical and Information Technology works broadly with research within cryptography, computer security, wireless and fixed networks. The division runs several research projects through different research collaboration platforms and arranges workshops and seminar series, making the research at the division visible to larger audiences. Cross-disciplinary projects are formed through our research collaboration platforms and national and international research networks, contributing to a dynamic research environment. We are now looking for a doctoral student to work within cybersecurity and software engineering in a project funded by the Swedish Foundation for Strategic Research, SSF. Being a doctoral student As a doctoral student, you are both admitted as a student and employed at Lund University. As a doctoral student, you will be trained in a scientific approach. In short, you will be trained to think critically and analytically, to solve problems independently using the right methods, and to develop an awareness of research ethics. In addition, you will have the opportunity to work on projects, to develop your leadership and pedagogical skills. Throughout your studies, you will be guided by supervisors. Doctoral studies end with a thesis and a doctoral degree. More about being a doctoral student at LTH on lth.se . Project description You will work in the RESIST project and primarily contribute to the area of ​​protection of distributed AI. RESIST is in collaboration with Linköping University, Örebro University and the research institute RISE and is a multidisciplinary research center that works with the rapidly growing area of ​​developing conditions and techniques to protect future AI-based systems. The center is led by Linköping University with Lund University as a partner. Work duties You will primarily devote yourself to your doctoral programme, which includes participation in research projects as well as third cycle courses, seminars and conferences. The main duties are to conduct research and teaching at the intersection of cyber-security and software engineering. The research focuses on automated principles for analysing program code. This includes how AI models can be used to analyze and correct software to ensure that it does not contain vulnerabilities. For example, it has been shown that AI-generated code often has significant security flaws. In order to use AI-generated code in high-security applications, the code generation process therefore needs to be made much more robust. The duties include evaluating existing techniques from a security perspective and identifying principles for automatic analysis and code correction. Case studies and experimental research are expected to be central research methods in the doctoral project. The duties also include participation in teaching and other departmental work (however, a maximum of 20% of working hours). Qualifications To be eligible for admission and employment as a doctoral student, you must fulfil the requirements below. Admission requirements A person meets the general admission requirements for third-cycle courses and study programmes if the applicant: has been awarded a second-cycle qualification, or has satisfied the requirements for courses comprising at least 240 credits of which at least 60 credits were awarded in the second cycle, or has acquired substantially equivalent knowledge in some other way. A person meets the specific admission requirements for third cycle studies in Electrical Engineering if the applicant has: at least 60 second-cycle credits in subjects of relevance to Electrical Engineering, or an MSc in Engineering in Biomedical Engineering, Computer Science, Electrical Engineering, Engineering Mathematics, Nanoengineering, Engineering Physics, or Information and Communication Engineering. Additional requirements In order to complete the doctoral programme in question, the following are also required: Very good software development skills. Experience of working with complex computer and communication systems. Good ability to work independently and to formulate and tackle research problems. Good written and oral communication skills. Good ability to cooperate. Very good knowledge of English, spoken and written. Other qualifications For the doctoral programme in question, the following are considered as other qualifications: Completed courses and/or master thesis work within Computer Security. Documented software engineering skills. Documented software development skills. Documented artificial intelligence technology skills. Documented teaching and communication skills. Industrial experience. We offer Lund University is a public authority, which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave, and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. Read more on the University website about being a Lund University employee Work at Lund University . About the employment The employment is a fixed-term employment at full time, starting as soon as possible by agreement, however no later than May 1, 2027. Third cycle studies at LTH consist of full-time studies for 4 years. In the case of teaching and other departmental duties, the employment is extended accordingly. Doctoral studentships are regulated in the Higher Education Ordinance (1993:100), chapter 5, 1-7 §§. More about terms of employment for doctoral students on Lund University’s Staffpages . How to apply Applications shall be written in English and include: CV and a cover letter stating the reasons why you are interested in the doctoral programme/employment and in what way the research project corresponds to your interests and educational background. Copies of issued study certificates and/or awarded degree certificates. These must confirm that you meet the general and specific admission requirements for the doctoral programme and show that you have the subject knowledge required for the doctoral programme project. Other documents you wish to be considered (grade transcripts, contact information for your references, letters of recommendation, etc.) We welcome your application. About the Faculty of Engineering LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment As soon as possible by agreement, however no later than May 1, 2027 Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2645 Contact Christian Gehrmann, Professor, +46462220000, christian.gehrmann@eit.lth.se Union representative SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 17.Sep.2026 Last application date 23.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:968020/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Electrical Engineering focusing on Applied Cryptography and Privacy",
      "description": "Doctoral student in Electrical Engineering focusing on Applied Cryptography and Privacy Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Doctoral student in Electrical Engineering focusing on Applied Cryptography and Privacy Login and apply Lund University, LTH, Electrical and Information Technology Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The Secure and Networked Systems Division within the Department of Electrical and Information Technology works broadly with research within Cryptography, Computer Security, Wireless and Fixed Networks. The security group within the Division has around 20 members. The main research directions are Cryptography, Privacy and System Security for Connected Systems. The division runs several research projects through different large research collaboration platforms and arranges extensive workshops and seminar series, making the research at the division visible to larger audiences. Cross-disciplinary projects are formed through our research collaboration platforms and national and international research networks, contributing to a dynamic research environment. Being a doctoral student As a doctoral student, you are both admitted as a student and employed at Lund University. As a doctoral student, you will be trained in a scientific approach. In short, you will be trained to think critically and analytically, to solve problems independently using the right methods and to develop an awareness of research ethics. In addition, you will have the opportunity to work on projects, to develop your leadership and pedagogical skills. Throughout your studies, you will be guided by supervisors. Doctoral studies end with a thesis and a doctoral degree. More about being a doctoral student at LTH on lth.se . Subject and project description Through the Wallenberg AI, Autonomous Systems and Software Program (WASP) we now offer a new doctoral student position in the field of Applied Cryptography and Privacy. WASP is Sweden’s largest individual research program ever, a major national initiative for strategically motivated basic research, education and faculty recruitment. The program addresses research on artificial intelligence and autonomous systems acting in collaboration with humans, adapting to their environment through sensors, information and knowledge, and forming intelligent systems-of-systems. More about WASP (wasp-sweden.org). The primary duties of the doctoral student positions are to perform research and teaching within the Applied Cryptography and Privacy area. The research is devoted to the broader area of privacy-preserving communication and computation outsourcing. Communication over the internet is susceptible to surveillance and censorship. Privacy preserving communication techniques (e.g., Tor, Nym, Snowflake) allow users to circumvent such surveillance and censorship. The research scope would include designing, analysing and implementing such systems; additionally, studying different attacks and countermeasures are expected to be part of the research method. Privacy-preserving computation outsourcing allows users to outsource computation tasks to a cloud server without revealing to the server anything about the user data or even what kind of computations the user is performing. There are different techniques for such privacy-preserving computation outsourcing such as Trusted Execution Environment (e.g., Intel SGX) and Fully Homomorphic Encryption (TFHE, BGV). Furthermore, the specific functions/tasks can be subject to attacks, and identifying attacks and countermeasures is expected to be studied. The research method will be a combination of system studies, design, and experimental research. Work duties You will primarily devote yourself to your doctoral programme, which includes participation in research projects as well as third cycle courses, seminars and conferences. The work duties include: Research in applied cryptography and privacy. Contributing to research projects. Taking doctoral students courses. The duties also include participation in teaching and other departmental work (however, a maximum of 20% of working hours). Qualifications To be eligible for admission and employment as a doctoral student, you must fulfil the requirements below. Admission requirements A person meets the general admission requirements for third-cycle courses and study programmes if the applicant: has been awarded a second-cycle qualification, or has satisfied the requirements for courses comprising at least 240 credits of which at least 60 credits were awarded in the second cycle, or has acquired substantially equivalent knowledge in some other way in Sweden or abroad. A person meets the specific admission requirements for third cycle studies in Electrical Engineering if the applicant has: at least 60 second-cycle credits in subjects of relevance to Electrical Engineering, or a MSc in Engineering in Biomedical Engineering, Computer Science, Electrical Engineering, Engineering Mathematics, Nanoengineering, Engineering Physics, or Information and Communication Engineering. Additional requirements In order to complete the doctoral programme in question, the following are also required: Good programming skills. Good ability to work independently and to formulate and tackle research problems. Good written and oral communication skills. Good ability to cooperate. Very good knowledge of English, spoken and written. Other qualifications For the doctoral programme in question, the following are considered as other qualifications: Completed courses and/or master thesis work within Computer Security and/or Cryptology. Industrial experience. Documented software development skills. Documented teaching and communication skills. We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. More about working at Lund University on lu.se . About the employment The employment is a fixed-term employment at full time, starting as soon as possible by agreement, however no later than May 1, 2027. Third cycle studies at LTH consist of full-time studies for 4 years. In the case of teaching and other departmental duties, the employment is extended accordingly. Doctoral studentships are regulated in the Higher Education Ordinance (1993:100), chapter 5, 1-7 §§. More about terms of employment for doctoral students on Lund University’s Staffpages . How to apply Applications shall be written in English and include: CV and a cover letter stating the reasons why you are interested in the doctoral programme/ employment and in what way the research project corresponds to your interests and educational background. Copies of issued study certificates and/or awarded degree certificates. These must confirm that you meet the general and specific admission requirements for the doctoral programme and show that you have the subject knowledge required for the doctoral programme project. Other documents you wish to be considered (grade transcripts, contact information for your references, letters of recommendation, etc.) We welcome your application. About the Faculty of Engineering LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time First day of employment As soon as possible by agreement, however no later than May 1, 2027 Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2647 Contact Debajyoti Das, Associate senior lecturer, +46462220000, debajyoti.das@eit.lth.se Union representative SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 17.Sep.2026 Last application date 23.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:970924/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Researcher in economic geography: infrastructure transitions (DECENT)",
      "description": "Researcher in economic geography: infrastructure transitions (DECENT) Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Researcher in economic geography: infrastructure transitions (DECENT) Login and apply Lunds universitet Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position is based at the Department of Human Geography (KEG) and affiliated with CIRCLE, Centre for Innovation Research (www.circle.lu.se). You will work in the research project DECENT (Socio-technical modularity and the decentralization of infrastructure), funded by the European Research Council through an ERC Starting Grant and running 2026–2031. DECENT asks why and how societal infrastructures – energy, water and sanitation, mobility, food provision – are becoming more decentralized, despite strong path dependencies favouring large-scale centralized systems. The project combines transition studies, modularity theory and economic geography and compares case studies in European and Indian cities. You will be part of a project team consisting of the principal investigator, two PhD students and yourself, with your workplace at Geocentrum I in Lund alongside the department's roughly 60 staff. At KEG and CIRCLE you join an international and interdisciplinary research environment in economic geography, innovation studies and sustainability transitions, with an open and supportive working culture in which junior and senior researchers collaborate closely. What we offer Lund University is a public authority, which means you receive particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension. We have a flexitime agreement that supports a good balance between work and leisure. You will have good conditions for research in a well-funded international project, with funding for fieldwork and conferences, and room to develop your own research profile. Read more about being an employee at Lund University: https://www.lu.se/en/about-university/work-us Work duties and areas of responsibility As a researcher in DECENT you work closely with the principal investigator and take independent responsibility for the research in one or more of the project's case studies. Your main duties are to: contribute to the project's theory development on decentralization and modularity in socio-technical systems, especially in the project's initial phase plan and carry out empirical work – document studies, interviews and fieldwork – in one or more infrastructure sectors (e.g. energy, water and sanitation, mobility or food) in European and Indian cities analyse the material using qualitative process methods (path tracing) and semi-quantitative network methods (socio-technical configuration analysis, STCA), and contribute to the project's methods development compare cases across sectors and across high- and middle-income contexts and contribute to the project's synthesis and theory building publish in international peer-reviewed journals, independently and together with the project team, and present at international conferences contribute to guiding the project's PhD students and to seminars, workshops and dialogue with practitioners and policy makers in the case-study contexts Travel and fieldwork, primarily in Europe and India, are part of the position. Qualifications Requirements for the position are: You hold a PhD in economic geography, human geography, innovation studies, transition studies or another social science discipline relevant to the position, such as economics. You have documented research experience in sustainability transitions, innovation or infrastructure (e.g. energy, water, mobility or food), demonstrated through publications in international peer-reviewed journals. You have experience of qualitative research involving your own data collection, for example interview-based case studies. You have excellent oral and written proficiency in English. Swedish is not a requirement. You have a strong ability to plan and drive research tasks independently, and good collaborative skills. Additional merits for the position are: You have experience of research on, and fieldwork in, middle-income countries, in particular India or other parts of South Asia. You have a good command of socio-technical transition theories, institutional theory and/or economic-geographical perspectives on transitions and agency. You have experience of research on firms, entrepreneurship or innovation ecosystems in infrastructure sectors. You have experience of research on justice and distributional aspects of infrastructure and energy transitions. You have experience of interdisciplinary collaboration and of engagement with policy actors and practitioners. You have experience of guiding early-career researchers or of applying for and leading externally funded projects. You have language skills relevant to fieldwork in the project's case-study contexts. In the selection, greatest weight is given to scientific proficiency demonstrated through publications and completed research, and to how well your research profile matches and complements the project's research questions. Other information The position is starting 1 January 2027 or by agreement. Interviews will be held in Lund shortly after the application deadline. For questions about the position, contact Johan Miörner (johan.miorner@keg.lu.se). How to apply Applications are submitted through the university's recruitment system. The application must be written in English and include: a letter (max 1 page) describing your research profile, how it relates to DECENT and how you would like to contribute to the project CV with a list of publications The Faculty of Social Sciences at Lund University is one of the leading education and research institutions in Sweden and operates both in Lund and Helsingborg. The Faculty of Social Sciences at Lund University is one of the leading education and research institutions in Sweden and operates both in Lund and Helsingborg. The Department of Human Geography (Institutionen för kulturgeografi och ekonomisk geografi) at Lund University is a research-intensive academic unit within the Faculty of Social Sciences. It combines long academic traditions with modern and highly internationalised research and education. The department hosts a broad portfolio of education at undergraduate, master’s and doctoral levels, including internationally oriented programmes such as the Master’s in Human Geography. The department comprises a diverse faculty. The exact staffing varies, but the department currently employs 55–60 academic and administrative staff. The department educates roughly 400 full-time students across bachelor’s and master’s programmes, and it employs about 15 doctoral students. The research environment is strongly oriented towards external funding and has been successful competitively at national and European levels. The department’s research and education activities have received very favourable assessments in internal and external evaluations, positioning it among Lund University’s leading disciplinary domains. Lund University as a whole performs prominently in global rankings. According to the most recent QS World University Rankings, Lund University is placed #72 globally and achieves strong subject-level positions, including in fields related to human geography and environmental sciences. Lund University also ranks #1 in the world for sustainability in the same ranking, reflecting its strengths in socially and environmentally relevant research and education. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Permanent position Contract type Full time First day of employment 1 januari 2027 (eller efter överenskommelse) Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2730 Contact Johan Miörner, 0462220000, johan.miorner@keg.lu.se Elin Johansson, 046222000, elin.johansson@sam.lu.se Union representative SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, 046-2220000, kansli@saco-s.lu.se Published 18.Sep.2026 Last application date 02.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:965225/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral position in translational lymphoma research",
      "description": "Postdoctoral position in translational lymphoma research Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Postdoctoral position in translational lymphoma research Login and apply Lund University Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The position will be located at the Department of Immunotechnology at Medicon Village in Lund and is part of CREATE Health and Lund University's Cancer Center . The Department of Immunotechnology conducts research ranging from advanced technology development to biomedical studies. The main research areas include sensitization, immuno-oncology, and biomarkers. The modern facilities are located at Medicon Village, where academic research is carried out side by side with private companies and actors from the healthcare sector. A number of important infrastructures are available, including specialized laboratories for antibody development, flow cytometry, spatial omics, and mass spectrometry, as well as NBIS (National Bioinformatics Infrastructure Sweden). The Department is also responsible for several student courses and offers high-quality education at both undergraduate and advanced levels. Approximately 40 people work within the Department. You will be part of a research group conducting translational lymphoma research with a focus on understanding tumor biology and the tumor microenvironment in lymphoid malignancies. Subject description The research focus of the position lies within translational lymphoma research, with particular emphasis on cutaneous T-cell lymphomas and high-grade B-cell lymphomas. The research group conducts experimental and translational studies using clinically well-characterized patient material in close collaboration with clinical researchers. The research aims to improve the biological understanding of lymphoma by characterizing tumor cells, immune cells, and their spatial organization within the tumor microenvironment. We use spatial transcriptomics and spatial proteomics, advanced image analysis, and computational approaches to investigate molecular and cellular heterogeneity and to integrate spatial molecular information with histopathological and clinical data. We are now seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral researcher with expertise in spatial omics, image analysis, and computational analysis of complex biological data. Work duties The main duties involved in a post-doctoral position is to conduct research. Teaching may also be included, but up to no more than 20% of working hours. The position includes the opportunity for three weeks of training in higher education teaching and learning. The purpose of the position is to develop independence as a researcher and to create the opportunity for further development. The postdoctoral position includes a combination of experimental work, data analysis, as well as interpretation and presentation of research results. The main part of the work for the advertised position involves translational studies of cutaneous T-cell lymphomas and high-grade B-cell lymphomas. Research will focus on spatial transcriptomic and spatial proteomic analysis of patient-derived tissue, quantitative image analysis, and integration of molecular, spatial, histopathological, and clinical data. Computational and statistical analyses will be performed primarily using R. The research is carried out in a multidisciplinary environment and in collaboration with clinical researchers and other relevant partners. Work duties include: Research within the subject area Teaching in the first, second and third cycles of studies Supervision of degree projects and doctoral students Actively seeking external research funding Collaboration with health care, industry and/or wider society Administration related to the work duties listed above Qualification requirements Appointment to a post-doctoral position requires that the applicant has a PhD, or an international degree deemed equivalent to a PhD, within the subject of the position. The certificate proving the qualification requirement is met, must be received before the employment decision is made. Priority will be given to candidates who have graduated no more than three years ago before the last day for application. Under special circumstances, the doctoral degree can have been completed earlier. Additional requirements: Very good oral and written proficiency in English. A completed doctoral degree in cell biology or immunology. Strong ability to initiate, develop and conduct high-quality research. Documented knowledge in lymphoma biology Documented practical experience in spatial transcriptomics and spatial proteomics. Documented practical experience in high-plex image analysis of tissue Documented knowledge and practical experience in programming and data analysis in R. Good communication skills and extensive documented experience of supervision of degree projects. Good collaborative skills, initiative and the ability to solve work tasks independently Assessment criteria This is a career development position primarily focused on research. The position is intended as an initial step in a career, and the assessment of the applicants will primarily be based on their research qualifications and potential as researchers. Particular emphasis will be placed on research skills within the subject. For appointments to a post-doctoral position, the following shall form the assessment criteria: A good ability to develop and conduct high quality research. Teaching skills. Other qualifications: Assessment of all the individual listed “additional requirements” above. Experience from industry within biotechnology, pharmaceuticals, diagnostics, or another relevant life-science field is considered a merit. Consideration will also be given to how the applicant’s experience and skills complement and strengthen ongoing research within the department, and how they stand to contribute to its future development. We offer Lund University is a public authority which means that employees get particular benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. It is advisable to enter information on what your unit specifically can offer as a workplace. Read more on the University website about being a Lund University employee Work at Lund University . Further information This is a full-time, fixed-term employment of 2 years, with a planned starting date of 1 January 2027. The period of employment is determined in accordance with the agreement “Avtal om tidsbegränsad anställning som postdoktor” (“Agreement on fixed-term employment as a post-doctoral fellow”). How to apply Applications are to be submitted via the University’s recruitment system. The application should include a CV and a personal letter justifying your interest in the position and how it matches your qualifications. The application should also include a degree certificate or equivalent and any other document to which you would like to draw attention (copies of grade transcripts, details of referees, letters of recommendation, etc.)” Welcome to apply! LTH is Lund University’s Faculty of Engineering. At LTH we educate people, build knowledge for the future and work hard for the development of society. We create space for brilliant research and inspire creative advancements in technology, architecture and design. We have nearly 12,000 students. Every year, our researchers – many of whom work in world-leading profile areas – publish around 100 theses and 2 000 scientific findings. In addition, a number of research results and degree projects are transformed into innovations. Together we explore and create – to benefit the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Contract type Full time Salary Månadslön Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2547 Union representative Published 18.Sep.2026 Last application date 02.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:958024/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "Project assistant",
      "description": "Project assistant at the Centre for Mathematical Sciences Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) Project assistant at the Centre for Mathematical Sciences Login and apply Lunds universitet, Naturvetenskapliga fakulteten, matematikcentrum Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Lund University, Centre for Mathematical Sciences, Division of Mathematics, Faculty of Science We are looking for employees to join an Erasmus+ funded project for developing teaching materials in mathematics education with programming and computation. Description of the workplace The Centre for Mathematical Sciences has approximately 150 employees, of which about 40 are in the Division of Mathematics, Faculty of Science (NF) where the position is located. Other departments are Mathematics LTH with numerical analysis and Mathematical statistics. The Division of Mathematics NF has the overall responsibility for the natural science bachelor's and master's programmes in mathematics and provides courses in mathematics and numerical analysis that are also available as electives in other programmes at the Faculty of Science and that can be studied as independent courses. We offer Lund University is a public authority that offers good employment conditions. Read more on the university's website about Working with us. Job assignments As an employee in the project, you will contribute to the development of teaching materials for mathematics education with programming, computation, and interactive digital tools. The tasks include, among other things: developing and testing tutorials, exercises, and projects that combine mathematics and programming. providing feedback on the pedagogical design of materials from a student perspective. implementing examples and solutions in programming languages (e.g., Python). learning and applying new technologies for interactive web-based tutorials (e.g., Jupyter Notebook). experimenting with and evaluating tools that link teaching materials to large language models (LLMs). collaborating with teachers and researchers in the project, as well as with international partner universities. Qualifications Requirements for the position are: a bachelor's degree in programming, computer science or equivalent . completed courses in mathematics at the Centre for Mathematical Sciences, Lund University (at least two semesters of full-time studies or equivalent). very good knowledge of spoken and written written English. good communication skills and the ability to work independently. Additional qualifications are: experience in teaching, tutoring, or the development of educational materials. own experience of using AI tools in mathematics studies. participation in conferences, workshops, or project work related to programming and AI in mathematics education. experience of international collaboration. Terms of employment The employment is a temporary position as a special fixed-term employee (SÄVA) in accordance with the Employment Protection Act (LAS). Scope: 20% per employee (two positions in total). Period: 2026-10-12 or as soon as possible thereafter until 2026-12-31, with some flexibility in the distribution of working hours. How to apply When you apply, please: attach a short personal letter (max. 1 A4 page). A CV does not need to be attached. describe your relevant education and experience, including courses in mathematics and programming. attach a transcript of records or other documentation that verifies your eligibility/qualifications. optionally provide examples of previous experiences in programming, teaching, or development of educational materials. optionally indicate a reference who can testify to your knowledge of mathematics/programming or your pedagogical ability. Within the Faculty of Science research and education is conducted within Astronomy, Biology, Physics, Geosciences, Chemistry, Mathematics, medical radiation physics, physical geography and Environmental Sciences. The Faculty of Science is organized into eight departments, gathered in the northern campus area in Lund. The Faculty of Science has approximately 1900 students, 330 PhD students and 730 employees. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Special fixed-term employment Contract type Part-time First day of employment 2026-10-12 or as soon as possible thereafter Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 2 Full-time equivalent 20 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2347 Contact Jan-Fredrik Olsen, Univ.lektor, +46462228632, jan-fredrik.olsen@math.lu.se Union representative OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362, st@st.lu.se SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, kansli@saco-s.lu.se, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366, sekocivil@seko.lu.se Published 18.Sep.2026 Last application date 02.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    },
    "https://lu.varbi.com/en/what:job/jobID:959446/type:job/where:4/apply:1": {
      "title": "PhD position in Systemic Radiotherpy",
      "description": "PhD position in Systemic Radiotherpy Login and apply Swedish Norwegian Danish Finnish German Dutch Chinese (BETA) Polish French Romanian Norwegian (nynorsk) PhD position in Systemic Radiotherpy Login and apply Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Sciences, Lund Lund University was founded in 1666 and is repeatedly ranked among the world’s top universities. The University has around 46 000 students and 8 500 staff based in Lund, Helsingborg and Malmö. We are united in our efforts to understand, explain and improve our world and the human condition. Description of the workplace The doctoral project will be part of the multidisciplinary research environment within MIPCART and the systemic radionuclide therapy activities at the Division of Oncology, Department of Clinical Sciences, Lund University. This interdisciplinary composition enables studies ranging from the development and preclinical evaluation of radiopharmaceuticals to biological characterization, quantitative image analysis, and assessment of treatment response. Within the research group, we value a positive work environment characterized by respect and consideration in our relationships. We continuously strive to create conditions that promote job satisfaction, development, and participation for all employees. The research group is now recruiting a full-time doctoral student (100%) with a start date of 1 October 2026 (or as agreed). Description of PhD Project The research focuses on the clinical and preclinical development of radionuclide therapy and advanced imaging methodologies, with particular emphasis on prostate cancer and other aggressive malignancies. The research group consists of researchers with complementary expertise in molecular biology, oncology, medical radiation physics, radionuclide therapy, radiochemistry, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), radiation dosimetry, and image analysis. We offer Lund University is a public authority, which means that employees get benefits, generous annual leave and an advantageous occupational pension scheme. Read more on the University website about being a Lund University employee: Work at Lund University Work description As a doctoral student, your primary responsibility will be to pursue third-cycle education through research projects aimed at developing and optimizing radionuclide therapy for prostate cancer and other malignancies. The doctoral training is carried out through participation in the various components of the projects, enabling the student to progressively acquire knowledge in scientific methodology, experimental research, data analysis, and scientific communication. As part of your doctoral studies, your duties will include: Participating in the planning and execution of preclinical studies of targeted radiopharmaceuticals. Working with experimental tumor models, including cell culture, establishment of tumor models, and monitoring and care of laboratory animals. Performing biological sample collection and contributing to histological and immunohistochemical analyses of biological markers. Analyzing microscopy images and other biological data. Analyzing and visualizing collected data using appropriate statistical methods and relevant software. Conducting literature reviews and completing doctoral-level coursework. Presenting research findings at seminars and scientific conferences. Writing scientific publications and a doctoral thesis. Following introduction and training, the PhD student is expected to gradually assume increasing responsibility for project planning, experimental execution, data analysis, and scientific reporting. Additional duties related to the position may be assigned. Qualifications Required Qualifications; A second-cycle degree (Master’s level) in Molecular Biology, Biomedicine, or another closely related field considered relevant to doctoral studies. Knowledge of cell and molecular biology, tumor biology, or a related area within molecular biology. Experience in molecular biology laboratory work. The ability and willingness to work with laboratory animals following the necessary training. Excellent written and spoken English communication skills. Good collaborative skills, particularly in interdisciplinary environments. Excellent written and spoken English communication skills. We expect you, as a person, to be meticulous and goal-oriented. Meritorious Qualifications are: Advanced courses in cancer biology, immunology, histology, pathology, molecular imaging, or radiation biology. Training in laboratory animal science or equivalent certification for animal research. Experience with preclinical prostate cancer models or other animal-based research. Experience in histological sample preparation, immunohistochemistry, immunofluorescence, microscopy, or related image analysis. Experience in working with ionizing radiation, such as handling open radiation sources or radioactive tissue samples. Knowledge of cancer biology, radionuclide therapy, radiation biology, or molecular imaging. Eligibility Students with basic eligibility for third-cycle studies are those who- have completed a second-cycle degree- have completed courses of at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits are from second-cycle courses, or- have acquired largely equivalent knowledge in some other way, in Sweden or abroad. The employment of doctoral students is regulated in the Swedish Code of Statues 1998: 80. Only those who are or have been admitted to PhD-studies may be appointed to doctoral studentships. When an appointment to a doctoral studentship is made, the ability of the student to benefit from PhD-studies shall primarily be taken into account. In addition to devoting themselves to their studies, those appointed to doctoral studentships may be required to work with educational tasks, research and administration, in accordance with specific regulations in the ordinance. Type of employment Limit of tenure, four years according to HF 5 kap 7§. How to apply Applications are to be submitted via the University’s recruitment system. The application should include a CV and a personal letter justifying your interest in the position and how it matches your qualifications. The application should also include a degree certificate or equivalent and any other document to which you would like to draw attention (copies of grade transcripts, details of referees, letters of recommendation, etc.). Welcome to apply! The Department of Clinical Sciences Lund is one of six departments at the Faculty of Medicine. Clinical Sciences Lund cooperates closely with Skåne University Hospital and the Faculty of Medicine in order to optimize the conditions for preclinical and clinical translational research, research strategies and development, as well as education on both undergraduate and postgraduate levels. The Faculty of Medicine is a part of Lund University, and is responsible for education and research within medicine and healthcare. Our academic programs are closely linked with the healthcare system and are firmly anchored in the faculty’s strong research tradition. Our research spans a broad field within experimental preclinical research, near-patient clinical research and health sciences research. The Faculty of Medicine, with its 1,800 employees and 2,700 students in Lund and Malmö, is a knowledge-intensive meeting place for students, teachers and researchers from all over the world. We kindly decline all sales and marketing contacts. Type of employment Temporary position Employment expires 2030-11-29 First day of employment 2026-12-01 Salary Monthly salary Number of positions 1 Full-time equivalent 100 City Lund County Skåne län Country Sweden Reference number PA2026/2387 Contact Lina Bull, HR-partner, lina.bull@med.lu.se Oskar Vilhelmsson Timmermand, oskar.vilhelmsson_timmermand@med.lu.se Union representative OFR/ST:Fackförbundet ST:s kansli, 046-2229362 SACO:Saco-s-rådet vid Lunds universitet, kansli@saco-s.lu.se SEKO: Seko Civil, 046-2229366 Published 18.Sep.2026 Last application date 09.Oct.2026 Login and apply Share links Return to job vacancies Grade Varbi Recruit recruitment system Cookies Terms Data privacy policy Accessibility statement × Modal title We use cookies for login, improved user experience, share links and in some cases marketing and collection of statistics. Accept all cookies Accept only necessary Read more about our cookies here ×"
    }
